\title{RF Chain Count to Mitigate Beam Split in Wideband Hybrid Arrays: From Far-Field to Near-Field
\author{Ahmed Hussain, Asmaa Abdallah~\IEEEmembership{Senior Member, IEEE}, Abdulkadir Celik,~\IEEEmembership{Senior Member, IEEE},  and Ahmed M. Eltawil,~\IEEEmembership{Senior Member, IEEE}
\thanks{Ahmed Hussain, Asmaa Abdallah, and Ahmed M. Eltawil are with the Computer, Electrical, and Mathematical Sciences and Engineering (CEMSE) Division, King Abdullah University of Science and Technology (KAUST), Thuwal, 23955-6900, KSA. Abdulkadir Celik is with the School of Electronics and Computer Science, University of Southampton, SO17 1BJ, UK.}
}
}
\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
Deploying large antenna arrays transitions wireless communication into the radiative near-field (NF), where spherical wavefronts enable beamfocusing in the joint angle-distance domain. However, wideband beamforming with large antenna arrays suffers from beam split across both spatial dimensions. Although fully digital arrays can eliminate this impairment, their prohibitive hardware cost and power consumption motivate the adoption of hybrid array architectures equipped with a limited number of radio-frequency (RF) chains. This paper investigates the minimum number of RF chains required to mitigate the beam split. We derive closed-form expressions for the required number of RF chains in fully-connected (FC) architectures and propose an algorithm for sub-connected (SC) architectures, considering both uniform linear arrays (ULAs) and uniform circular arrays (UCAs). Our analysis reveals that beyond a focal distance of $10L$, where $L$ denotes the aperture length, the NF RF chain count converges to its far-field counterpart and becomes a function of the angle alone. Moreover, the FC hybrid architecture mitigates the beam split by using fewer RF chains than the SC architecture, albeit at the cost of increased complexity in the phase shifter network.
\end{abstract}
\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Near-field, wideband beamforming, large antenna arrays, bandwidth, beam split, hybrid arrays, RF chains, ULA, UCA.
\end{IEEEkeywords}
\section{Introduction} \label{sec-I}
\IEEEPARstart{A}{nalog} \acp{PS} apply a frequency-independent phase shift, whereas the propagation phase across an antenna array is frequency-dependent \cite{11050910}. Thus, an analog beamformer tuned to the carrier frequency becomes misaligned as the operating frequency changes, causing a loss of beamforming gain. In the \ac{FF}, this mismatch causes the beam to drift toward different angles across the signal bandwidth, a phenomenon known as \emph{beam squint} \cite{10934779}. The effect becomes more pronounced as signal bandwidth and array aperture increase, making beam squint a challenge in wideband \ac{mmWave} systems \cite{11050910,10220205}.
As the aperture becomes electrically large, communication transitions from the \ac{FF} to the radiative \ac{NF}. Consequently, conventional beamsteering evolves into \emph{beamfocusing}, where the array concentrates energy at a spatial location rather than merely along a direction \cite{10934779,10220205}. In this regime, the wideband counterpart of squint is \emph{beam split} \cite[Fig.~5]{9957130}, characterized by beam deviations in both the angle and distance dimensions. While \ac{TTD} beamforming can compensate for these impairments by introducing a frequency-dependent phase that tracks the propagation delay \cite{10541333,10458958}, the required delay ranges for large arrays are difficult to realize at \ac{mmWave} frequencies \cite{9833249}. Furthermore, analog \ac{TTD} networks incur substantial hardware complexity, power consumption, and cost \cite{9833249,10018114}. Hybrid architectures provide a practical alternative by operating between the fully analog and fully digital extremes, utilizing a limited number of \ac{RF} chains. The \ac{RF} chain count dictates the supported bandwidth for a given aperture and beamforming gain. This motivates the central question of this paper: \emph{What is the minimum number of \ac{RF} chains required by a wideband hybrid array to mitigate the \ac{NF} beam split?}
\subsection{Related Work} \label{subsec:related}
The spatial wideband effect manifests as inter-symbol interference in the time domain, loss of beamforming gain in the frequency domain, and the beam split in the spatial domain. Although a fully digital implementation can overcome these impairments through baseband processing, its hardware complexity scales directly with array size, rendering it impractical for massive arrays. Existing low-complexity architectures instead approximate the fully digital ideal through bandwidth-slicing or subarray-based designs \cite{9398864,lu2026wideband,10934783,631557,hussain2025near}. Bandwidth slicing partitions the operational bandwidth into narrow subbands, each with an independent phase-shift vector, because beam squint is negligible over sufficiently narrow frequency intervals \cite{9398864}. Alternatively, the array itself is partitioned into subarrays equipped with \acp{PS}, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:subarray_types}. In the simplest configuration, \acp{PS} are employed at the subarray level, which limits the array's scanning capability. The \ac{DPP} architecture \cite{10541333,9957130,10934783} improves upon this design by placing \acp{PS} at the element level and \acp{TTD} at the subarray level, which mitigates the scan limitation while containing the cost, since the \acp{TTD} are not deployed per element. Nevertheless, as highlighted earlier, large bandwidths and apertures require a wide range of delay values, which renders the \ac{TTD} design itself challenging. Hybrid beamforming architectures, encompassing both \ac{FC} and \ac{SC} configurations, leverage \ac{RF} chains to mitigate spatial wideband effects. While the minimum \ac{RF} chain requirement to suppress \ac{FF} beam squint in \ac{FC} arrays was derived in \cite{10495774}, that framework cannot be directly applied to the \ac{NF}, where beam split manifests across both angular and radial dimensions.
 The array geometry governs the beamfocusing characteristics of \ac{NF} arrays. Unlike the \ac{FF}, the \ac{NF} beams possess a finite beamdepth that enables both angle and range estimation \cite{wei2025fundamental}. For a fixed aperture length, the \ac{UCA} provides a narrower beamdepth and a larger effective \ac{NF} region, quantified by the \ac{EBRD}, than the \ac{ULA} \cite{11498324}. Moreover, \acp{UCA} achieve superior wideband sensing performance compared with \acp{ULA} of the same aperture \cite{10988576}. These observations suggest that the array geometry may also influence the \ac{RF}-chain requirement for beam split mitigation, motivating a comparison between \acp{ULA} and \acp{UCA}.
The operational bandwidth specifies the frequency range over which the system operates. In contrast, the \ac{IBW} denotes the frequency interval over which an antenna array preserves a prescribed beamforming gain \cite{haupt2015timed,brown2021active}. Conceptually, the \ac{IBW} is the frequency-domain analogue of beamwidth and beamdepth, which define the angle and range intervals, respectively, over which the beamforming gain exceeds a specified threshold. In the \ac{FF}, the \ac{IBW} metric is utilized to determine the number of \ac{RF} chains specifically for \ac{SC} architectures \cite{brown2021active}. However, the corresponding \ac{NF} characterization remains unavailable, as the dependence of the \ac{IBW} on both angle and range has not yet been established. This motivates the derivation of analytical expressions for the \ac{NF} \ac{IBW}, together with closed-form design rules for the required \ac{RF} chain count.
\subsection{Main Contributions}
To address the research gaps highlighted in Section \ref{subsec:related}, the main contributions of this paper are summarized as follows.
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{NF radial beam split boundary}: We adopt a hybrid beamforming architecture and characterize the frequency-dependent beamforming gain in the distance domain. We decouple \ac{NF} beam split into an angular component, which coincides with \ac{FF} beam squint, and a radial component exclusive to the \ac{NF}. Furthermore, we derive the boundary beyond which the radial beam split vanishes.
\item \textbf{RF chain count for SC hybrid arrays}: We derive closed-form \ac{NF} \ac{IBW} expressions for the \ac{ULA} and the \ac{UCA}. By applying the \ac{IBW} of the binding subarray, we propose an algorithm that determines the required \ac{RF} chain count for \ac{SC} hybrid architectures.
\item \textbf{RF chain count for FC hybrid arrays}: To determine the required \ac{RF} chain count for \ac{FC} hybrid arrays in the \ac{NF}, we analyze the eigenspectrum of the space-frequency correlation matrix. The number of dominant eigenvalues simplifies to the product of the bandwidth and the delay spread across the aperture, yielding the \ac{RF} chain requirements for both the \ac{ULA} and \ac{UCA}.
\item \textbf{NF vs. FF RF chain comparison}: Numerical results demonstrate that the \ac{NF} \ac{RF} chain requirement becomes range-invariant beyond a focal distance of $10L$ \footnote{Boresight is an exception for very large bandwidth.}, where $L$ is the aperture length, coinciding with the \ac{FF} \ac{RF} chain count. Furthermore, \ac{FC} architectures require fewer \ac{RF} chains than \ac{SC} architectures, but at the cost of a more complex phase shifter network.
\end{itemize}
\subsection{Notation and Organization of the Paper}
Matrices, vectors, and scalars are denoted by bold uppercase, bold lowercase, and lowercase letters, respectively. The transpose and conjugate transpose are denoted by $(\cdot)^{\mathrm{T}}$ and $(\cdot)^{{\mathsf{H}}}$, respectively. The Euclidean ($\ell_2$) norm of a vector is denoted by $\|\cdot\|$, the absolute value of a scalar by $|\cdot|$, the ceiling operator by $\lceil\cdot\rceil$, and the trace of a square matrix by $\operatorname{tr}(\cdot)$. 
The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec-II} presents the system model. Section~\ref{sec-III} characterizes the beam split and \ac{IBW} for the \ac{ULA}. Section~\ref{sec-IV} derives the minimum \ac{RF} chain count to mitigate beam split across both \ac{SC} and \ac{FC} architectures for \ac{ULA}. Section~\ref{sec-V} extends this framework to the \ac{UCA}. Section~\ref{sec-VI} presents numerical results to validate the analysis, and Section~\ref{sec-VII} concludes the paper.
