\section{System Model} \label{sec-II}
We consider a downlink communication system where the \ac{BS} employs a large $N$-element antenna array oriented along the $x$-axis. We adopt a free-space \ac{LoS} \ac{NF} channel model. Denoting the carrier frequency by $f_c$ and the frequency offset relative to it by $f\in[-\tfrac{W}{2},\tfrac{W}{2}]$, with $W$ the bandwidth, the $\nth{n}$ entry of the \ac{NF} array response vector $\mathbf{a}(f) \in \mathbb{C}^{N} $ for a \ac{UE} located at a distance $r$ with respect to the array center is
\begin{equation}
[\mathbf{a}(f)]_n=\exp\!\Big(-2\pi j\frac{f_c+f}{c}\,\big(d(x_n)-r\big)\Big),
\label{eq:steer}
\end{equation}
where $d(x_n)$ denotes the distance from the \ac{UE} to the $\nth{n}$ element of the antenna array. The amplitude factor omitted in \eqref{eq:steer} is absorbed into the \ac{SNR} \cite{10495774}.
Furthermore, amplitude variation across different antenna elements is assumed negligible. 
We consider a hybrid beamforming architecture with $K$ \ac{RF} chains. The \ac{BS} employs a frequency-flat analog beamformer $\mathbf{W}_a\in\mathbb{C}^{N\times K}$ followed by a frequency-dependent digital beamformer $\mathbf{w}_d(f)\in\mathbb{C}^{K}$. The received signal
corresponding to the transmit symbol $q(f)$ is
\begin{equation}
y(f)=\mathbf{a}^{\mathsf{H}}(f)\mathbf{W}_a\mathbf{w}_d(f)\,q(f)+v(f),
\label{eq:received_signal}
\end{equation}
where $v(f)$ is additive white Gaussian noise with unit variance. The \emph{beamforming gain} at frequency $f$ is
\begin{equation}
g(f)=\frac{\big|\mathbf{a}^{\mathsf{H}}(f)\mathbf{W}_a\mathbf{w}_d(f)\big|^2}
{\big\|\mathbf{W}_a\mathbf{w}_d(f)\big\|^2}.
\label{eq:gf}
\end{equation}
For a fixed analog stage $\mathbf{W}_a$, we can find the optimal digital beamformer using the \ac{LS} estimator as \cite{7448873}
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{w}_d(f) = (\mathbf{W}_a^{\mathsf{H}}\mathbf{W}_a)^{-1}\mathbf{W}_a^{\mathsf{H}}\mathbf{a}(f),
\label{eq:gf_quot}
\end{equation}
which bounds the maximum beamforming gain as
\begin{equation}
g(f) = \big\|(\mathbf{W}_a^{\mathsf{H}}\mathbf{W}_a)^{-1/2}\mathbf{W}_a^{\mathsf{H}}\mathbf{a}(f)\big\|^2.
\label{eq:gf_bound}
\end{equation}
Furthermore, the average beamforming gain is defined as 
\begin{equation}
g_{\mathrm{av}}=\frac{1}{W}\int_{-W/2}^{W/2}g(f)\,df,
\label{eq:gavg} 
\end{equation}
which is averaged over the bandwidth $W$.
\section{Beam Split and Instantaneous Bandwidth} \label{sec-III}
We consider a \ac{BS} equipped with a \ac{ULA} with inter-element spacing ($\Delta=\lambda/2$) and aperture length\footnote{The aperture length refers to the largest physical dimension of the array. Specifically, it corresponds to the total length for a \ac{ULA} and the diameter for a \ac{UCA}.} $L \approx N\Delta$. A single-antenna \ac{UE} is located at a distance $r$ from the array center and an angle $\varphi$ measured with respect to the array normal. The position of the $\nth{n}$ antenna element is given by $x_n = \left(n-\frac{N}{2}\right)\Delta$, and the corresponding element--\ac{UE} distance is
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
d(x_n) &=\sqrt{r^2-2rx_n\sin\varphi+x_n^2} \label{eq:fresnel_exact} 
&\overset{(a)}{\approx}r-x_n\sin\varphi+\frac{x_n^2\cos^2\varphi}{2r}, 
\label{eq:fresnel}
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
where $(a)$ follows from the Fresnel approximation \cite{10988573,11428208}. The \ac{FF} corresponds to $r\to\infty$, where the quadratic term vanishes, and the wavefront becomes planar. The classical boundary between \ac{NF} and \ac{FF} is given by the Rayleigh distance $\RD = \frac{2L^2}{\lambda}$ \cite{11095387}. In \eqref{eq:fresnel}, the linear term determines the beam direction, while the quadratic term encodes the wavefront curvature associated with the focusing distance.
Beam split in the \ac{NF} across both angle and distance can be observed from the beamforming gain $g(f)$ in \eqref{eq:gf} on the space--frequency plane. To expose the beam split, we omit the digital stage and fix the analog beamformer with a single \ac{RF} chain $\mathbf{w}_a$ at the carrier frequency, which reduces \eqref{eq:gf} to
\begin{equation}
g(f)=\big|\mathbf{w}^{\mathsf{H}}_a\mathbf{a}(f)\big|^2 .
\label{eq:g_analog}
\end{equation}
Let the \ac{NF} beam be focused towards $(\varphi_0,r_0)$ at the carrier frequency $f_c$. Combining \eqref{eq:steer}--\eqref{eq:fresnel} with \eqref{eq:g_analog}, the gain becomes
\begin{equation}
g(f)=\left|\sum_{n=1}^{N}
\exp\!\left(-\,j\frac{2\pi}{c}\,\Xi_n(f)\right)\right|^2,
\label{eqn12}
\end{equation}
where the per-element phase mismatch is
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\Xi_n(f) &= \underbrace{\frac{x^2_n}{2}\!\left(f_c\frac{\cos^2\varphi_0}{r_0}
-(f_c+f)\frac{\cos^2\varphi}{r}\right)}_{\text{curvature (distance) term}} 
&\quad - \underbrace{x_n\big(f_c\sin\varphi_0-(f_c+f)\sin\varphi\big)}_{\text{tilt (angle) term}} .
\end{split}
\label{eq:Xi}
\end{equation}
To evaluate the beam split, the coefficients of the linear and quadratic terms in \eqref{eq:Xi} are set to zero. Each condition is subsequently analyzed in turn.
\subsection{Angular Beam Split}
For the frequency-dependent propagation phase to perfectly match the fixed analog phase, the tilt term in \eqref{eq:Xi} requires
\begin{equation}
(f_c+f)\sin\varphi=f_c\sin\varphi_0
 \ \Longrightarrow \ 
\sin\varphi=\frac{1}{(1+\bar{f})}\,\sin\varphi_0 ,
\label{eq:squint_angle}
\end{equation}
where $\bar{f} = \frac{f}{f_c}$ is normalized frequency offset. The beam, therefore, points at a different angle at every frequency, other than the carrier. Since $f\in[-\tfrac{W}{2},+\tfrac{W}{2}]$, the peak drifts \emph{toward} boresight for $f>0$ and \emph{away} from boresight for $f<0$. This frequency-dependent angular drift is referred to as \emph{angular beam split},\textbf{ which vanishes only at boresight ($\varphi_0=0$) and becomes more pronounced as the beam is steered toward larger angles}.
\subsection{Radial Beam Split}
Similarly, matching the frequency-dependent term to the fixed analog phase in the curvature component of \eqref{eq:Xi} requires
\begin{equation}
(f_c+f)\,\frac{\cos^2\varphi}{r}=f_c\,\frac{\cos^2\varphi_0}{r_0},
 \implies 
r= r_0 \, ({1+\bar{f}}) \,\frac{\cos^2\varphi}{\cos^2\varphi_0}.
\label{eq:split_match}
\end{equation}
By substituting \eqref{eq:squint_angle} into \eqref{eq:split_match}, the effect of angular beam split is removed, and \eqref{eq:split_match} simplifies to
\begin{equation}
r=\xi(\bar f,\varphi_0) r_0,
\qquad
\xi(\bar f,\varphi_0)=\frac{(1+\bar f)^2-\sin^2\varphi_0}
{(1+\bar f)\cos^2\varphi_0}.
\label{eq:split_range}
\end{equation}
This frequency-dependent focal-range drift is \emph{radial beam split}. The focal range drifts \emph{outward} ($r>r_0$) for $f>0$ and \emph{inward} ($r<r_0$)
for $f<0$. 
\subsection{NF Radial Beam Split Boundary}
The angular beam split vanishes at boresight, whereas the radial beam split is confined to the \ac{NF}. We use the beamdepth expression to determine the boundary within the \ac{NF} beyond which the radial beam split becomes negligible.
\subsubsection{Beamdepth at $f_c+f$}\label{sec-IIIC1}
The beamdepth is defined as the distance interval over which the array gain remains within $3$\,dB of its peak value \cite{10443535}. For a beam focused at distance $r_0$ at the carrier frequency $f_c$, the beamdepth is given by \cite{10934779}
\begin{equation} 
\mathrm{BD} = \frac{8\,\etarange^2 \,r_0^{2}\,\RD\cos^2\varphi_0}{(\RD \cos^2\varphi_0)^2-(4\,\etarange^2\,r_0)^2},
\label{eq:bd}
\end{equation}
where $\etarange = 1.318$, assuming a 3 dB loss threshold, $\rho_\epsilon=0.5$. Substituting the drifted range
\eqref{eq:split_range} for $r_0$, the squint angle \eqref{eq:squint_angle}
for $\varphi_0$, and the Rayleigh distance at the offset frequency
$(1+\bar f)\RD$ for $\RD$, the beamdepth at $f_c+f$ becomes
\begin{equation} 
\overline{\mathrm{BD}} = \xi(\bar f,\varphi_0)\, \mathrm{BD}.
\label{eq:bd_split}
\end{equation}
Beam split shifts the focal range and scales the beamdepth by the same factor. At boresight $\xi=1+\bar f$, and the beamdepth widens by
$(1+\bar f)$ for $f>0$ and narrows by $(1-|\bar f|)$ for $f<0$. Away from boresight $\xi$ carries the additional factor $\cos^2\varphi/\cos^2\varphi_0$, which exceeds unity for $f>0$. The monotonicity of $\xi$ in $\bar f$ makes this behavior consistent at all steering angles, with the beamdepth increasing as the focus shifts away from the array.
