\subsubsection{Radial Beam Split Boundary}
The \ac{NF} beam split boundary, denoted as $r_{\mathrm{sp}}$, is the distance beyond which the radial beam split ceases to act in the \ac{NF}, leaving angular beam split as the sole impairment. It is obtained by setting the denominator of the beamdepth \eqref{eq:bd} to zero, and is given by
\begin{equation}
r_{\mathrm{sp}}=\frac{{\RD}\cos^2\varphi_0}{4\,\etarange^2}.
\label{eq:nfbb}
\end{equation}
\begin{proof}
As the focusing range $r_0$ increases, the denominator of the beamdepth expression \eqref{eq:bd_split}, which is the same as that in \eqref{eq:bd}, shrinks and the beamdepth increases. The boundary at which the beamdepth becomes infinite marks the range beyond which the focal drift can no longer be resolved, so that the radial beam split no longer reduces the gain. Setting the denominator of \eqref{eq:bd} to zero
\begin{equation}
(\RD\cos^2\varphi_0)^2-(4\,\etarange^2\,r_0)^2=0,
\label{eq:nfbb_denom}
\end{equation}
and solving for the positive root yields \eqref{eq:nfbb}. 
\end{proof}
Radial beam split in \eqref{eq:split_range} is most severe at short distances where the wavefront curvature across the aperture is pronounced. \textbf{It weakens with range, and vanishes at the beam split boundary $r_{\mathrm{sp}}$ in \eqref{eq:nfbb}}. This boundary coincides with the \ac{EBRD} boundary \cite{11428208,10443535}, which defines the maximum beamfocusing limit. Beyond this limit, the array can no longer resolve in the distance dimension, and the radial beam split disappears. Only the angular beam split remains for $r_0 > r_{\mathrm{sp}}$. To exemplify this, Fig.~\ref{fig:bd_ebrd} presents the distance-domain gain of a beam focused at boresight at $f_c$ and evaluated at the two band edges. At $r_0=0.02\,\RD$, the beamdepth for the upper band edge ($f_c+W/2$) shifts to the right and widens, while for the lower band edge ($f_c-W/2$), it shifts to the left and contracts. At $r_0=r_{\mathrm{sp}}=0.14\,\RD$, the right-side half-power boundary is absent, and the gain decays monotonically toward $\rho_\epsilon$ without crossing it. Consequently, beams have infinite depth at this range, as predicted by \eqref{eq:nfbb}. Finally, we highlight that the angular beam split is the same in the \ac{FF} and the \ac{NF}. This behavior mirrors the beamwidth, which also remains the same in the \ac{NF} and the \ac{FF} \cite{11428208}.
\subsection{Instantaneous Bandwidth}\label{subsec:ula_ibw}
In the \ac{NF}, angular and radial beam split drift the focal point, according to \eqref{eq:squint_angle} and \eqref{eq:split_range}, respectively. Both effects scale with the offset $f$ from the carrier frequency $f_c$ and reduce the beamforming gain at the focal point. The \ac{IBW} is then defined as the frequency deviation $f$ from the carrier frequency at which this gain reduces to an acceptable threshold $\rho_\epsilon$ \cite{9020885, haupt2015timed, brown2021active}. In electromagnetics, a 3dB threshold $\rho_\epsilon = 0.5$ is regarded as an acceptable threshold, most commonly used for beamwidth and beamdepth \cite{hansen2009phased,10988573,10934779,11428208,10443535}. 
To isolate the array-gain degradation caused solely by frequency offset in \eqref{eq:Xi}, we evaluate the phase mismatch at the beamfocusing point by setting $\varphi=\varphi_0$, $r=r_0$. Under these conditions, the per-element phase mismatch simplifies to $\Xi_n=- f\,\delta_n$, where $\delta_n=\frac{x^2_n\cos^2\varphi_0}{2r_0}-x_n\sin\varphi_0$. The normalized form of the gain expression in \eqref{eqn12} is then given by
\begin{equation}\small
g(f)=\left| \frac{1}{N} \sum_{n=1}^{N}
\exp\!\left(j\,\frac{2\pi f}{c}\,\delta_n\right)\right|^2,
\label{eqn-ibw-master}
\end{equation}
\normalsize
whose tilt term $x_n\sin\varphi_0$ and curvature term $\tfrac{x^2_n\cos^2\varphi_0}{2r_0}$ determine the angle-domain \ac{IBW} and the distance-domain \ac{IBW}, respectively. 
\subsubsection{Angle-Domain IBW}
Retaining the tilt term $x_n\sin\varphi_0$ of \eqref{eqn-ibw-master}, the angle domain gain $g_a(f)$ is given by
\begin{align}\small
g_a(f)
&=\left|\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=1}^{N}
\exp\!\left(-\,j\,\frac{2\pi f}{c}\,x_n\sin\varphi_0\right)\right|^2 ,
&=\left|\frac{1}{N}\,
\frac{\sin\!\big(\pi N \Delta\, f\sin\varphi_0/c\big)}
{\sin\!\big(\pi \Delta\, f\sin\varphi_0/c\big)}\right|^2 .
\label{eqn-af-closed}
\end{align}
\normalsize
For large $N$ the denominator argument stays small across the mainlobe and \eqref{eqn-af-closed} reduces to $\operatorname{sinc}^2 (\etaangle)$ and
$\etaangle=\pi N\Delta f\sin\varphi_0/c$. To find \ac{IBW} at a specific threshold $\rho_\epsilon$, we set $g_a(\etaangle) = \rho_\epsilon$, which evaluates to $\etaangle \approx 1.391$ for $\rho_\epsilon = 0.5$. We can solve for one-sided frequency offset based on the $\etaangle$ as
\begin{equation}
\frac{\pi L f\sin\varphi_0}{c}=\etaangle,
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
f=\frac{\etaangle}{\pi}\,\frac{c}{L\,\sin\varphi_0}.
\label{eqn-deltaf-angle}
\end{equation}
The angle-domain \ac{IBW} is twice the one-sided frequency offset
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{IBW}^{\mathrm{ula}}_{a}
=2f
=\frac{2\etaangle}{\pi}\,\frac{c}{L\,\sin\varphi_0}
\approx\frac{0.886\,c}{L\,\sin\varphi_0},
\label{eqn-ula-ibw-angle}
\end{equation}
which depends on the aperture length $L$ and the steering angle $\varphi_0$. It decreases as $L$ increases and as the beam is steered away from the boresight. At boresight ($\varphi_0=0$), it becomes unbounded, consistent with \eqref{eq:squint_angle}, which predicts the absence of angular beam split.
\subsubsection{Distance-Domain IBW}
Retaining the curvature term of \eqref{eqn-ibw-master}, the distance domain gain $g_r(f)$ is given by
\begin{equation}\small
g_r(f) =\left|\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=1}^{N}
\exp\!\left(j\,\frac{\pi x^2_n\cos^2\varphi_0}{c}\,\frac{f}{r_0}\right)\right|^2 .
\label{eqn-gr-sum}
\end{equation}
\normalsize
Approximating the sum by its integral over $n$ recasts \eqref{eqn-gr-sum} as
\begin{equation}\small
g_r(\gamma)
\approx\frac{\mathcal{C}^2(\gamma)+\mathcal{S}^2(\gamma)}{\gamma^2},
\qquad
\gamma=\frac{L\cos\varphi_0}{2}\sqrt{\frac{2\,f}{c\,r_0}} . 
\label{eqn-gr-fresnel}
\end{equation}
\normalsize
Here, $\mathcal{C}(\cdot)$ and $\mathcal{S}(\cdot)$ denote Fresnel cosine and sine integrals \cite{10443535}. We define $\etarange \triangleq \gamma$ and find $\etarange$ such that $g_r(\etarange)=\rho_\epsilon$, which evaluates to $\etarange \approx1.318$ for $\rho_\epsilon=0.5$. Solving $\etarange = \gamma$ for the two-sided frequency offset yields 
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{IBW}^{\mathrm{ula}}_{r}
=2f
=\frac{4\,\etarange^2\,c\,r_0}{L^2\cos^2\varphi_0}
\approx\frac{7\,c\,r_0}{L^2\cos^2\varphi_0}.
\label{eqn-ula-ibw-range}
\end{equation}
The distance-domain \ac{IBW} increases with the range $r_0$ and with the angle $\varphi_0$ through the factor $\cos^2\varphi_0$. This behavior contrasts with the angle-domain \ac{IBW} in \eqref{eqn-ula-ibw-angle}, which decreases at large angles. The distance-domain \ac{IBW} is linked to radial beam split, a direct consequence of the \ac{NF} effect, which is strongest at boresight and weakest at endfire for a \ac{ULA} \cite{10988573}.
\subsubsection{Combined Near-Field IBW}
The angle-domain and distance-domain \acp{IBW} exhibit opposite trends with respect to angle. At practical distances, one of the two is the limiting factor over most of the angular interval. Consequently, the \ac{NF} \ac{IBW} is approximated by the minimum of the two, given by
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{IBW}^{\mathrm{ula}}
=\min\!\left(\frac{0.886c}{L\sin\varphi_0},\, 
\frac{7c\,r_0}{L^2\cos^2\varphi_0}\right).
\label{eqn-ula-ibw-min}
\end{equation}
The two branches intersect at the crossover distance $r_0 = \frac{L\cos^2\varphi_0}{7.84\sin\varphi_0}$. Radial beam split dominates below this threshold, while angular beam split governs performance above it. The derived expression is accurate in regimes where one effect dominates, but it overestimates the \ac{IBW} near the crossover point where both branches contribute equally. 
