\section{RF Chain Count for Hybrid ULA}\label{sec-IV}
This section derives the required \ac{RF} chain count to mitigate the beam split for \ac{SC} and \ac{FC} hybrid \ac{ULA}. In the \ac{SC} architecture, the block-diagonal analog precoder decomposes the wideband beamforming gain into independent subarray contributions, allowing the required partitioning to be determined directly from the localized \ac{IBW} criterion developed in Section~\ref{subsec:ula_ibw}. In contrast, the dense \ac{FC} analog precoder spans the entire aperture, which instead necessitates an eigenvalue analysis of the space-frequency correlation matrix.
\subsection{Sub-Connected Hybrid ULA}\label{sec-IVA}
The angular and distance \ac{IBW} in \eqref{eqn-ula-ibw-angle} and \eqref{eqn-ula-ibw-range} scale as $1/L$ and $1/L^{2}$, respectively, implying that a shorter aperture supports a wider bandwidth \cite{brown2021active,haupt2015timed}. Partitioning the array into subarrays reduces each subarray's aperture length, thereby increasing its \ac{IBW} and transforming the array-wide bandwidth requirement into a per-subarray \ac{IBW} constraint.
The whole array is partitioned into $K$ contiguous subarrays ${\mathcal{A}}_1,\dots,{\mathcal{A}}_K$, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:SC_block_diagram}. Each subarray has aperture length $L_k=L/K$ and comprises $N_k=N/K$ elements, with inter-subarray spacing $\Delta_s=N_k\Delta$. The $\nth{k}$ subarray is centered at ${x}_k^c$ and observes the global focal point $(\varphi_0,r_0)$ at a local angle $\varphi_k$ and range $r_k$. The local range $r_k$ from the focal point to the center of the subarray ${x}_k^c$ is obtained as 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:r_k}
r_k=d({x}_k^c),
\end{equation}
where $d(\cdot)$ is defined in \eqref{eq:fresnel_exact} and $\varphi_k$ can be obtained geometrically from Fig. \ref{fig:SC_block_diagram} as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:phi_k}
\sin\varphi_k=\frac{r_0\sin\varphi_0-{x}_k^c}{r_k}.
\end{equation}
The analog precoder in \eqref{eq:received_signal} for the \ac{SC} hybrid array is the block-diagonal matrix, given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:precoder_wa}
\mathbf{W}_a=\operatorname{blkdiag}(\mathbf{w}_1,\dots,\mathbf{w}_K)\in\mathbb{C}^{N\times K},
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{w}_k$ denotes the analog beamforming vector of the $\nth{k}$ subarray. The analog precoder is designed to compensate for the propagation delay to the focal point $(\varphi_0,r_0)$ at the carrier frequency $f_c$, yielding
\begin{equation}\label{eq:precoder_wk}
[\mathbf{W}_a]_{n,k} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{N_k}} e^{-j2\pi f_c\left(d(x_n)-r_0\right)/c},
\quad n\in {\mathcal{A}}_k.
\end{equation}
Since the subarrays occupy disjoint sets of antennas and each beamforming vector has unit norm, the Gram matrix reduces to the identity, $\mathbf{W}_a^{\mathsf{H}}\mathbf{W}_a=\mathbf{I}_K$. Consequently, the digital precoder in \eqref{eq:gf_quot} simplifies to the matched filter $\mathbf{w}_d(f)=\mathbf{W}_a^{\mathsf{H}}\mathbf{a}(f)$. The resulting beamforming gain in \eqref{eq:gf_bound} decomposes into the sum of the individual subarray gains
\begin{align}
g(f) &=\big\|\mathbf{W}_a^{{\mathsf{H}}}\mathbf{a}(f)\big\|^{2}
=\sum_{k=1}^{K} N_k\,g_k(f), \label{eq:gsc}
\text{where}, \quad g_k(f)
&=\left|
\frac{1}{N_k}
\sum_{n\in {\mathcal{A}}_k}
e^{-j2\pi f\left(d(x_n)-r_k\right)/c}
\right|^{2}, \label{eq:gkf}
\end{align}
is the normalized gain of the $\nth{k}$ subarray. The digital precoder removes the inter-subarray delay at every frequency. The residual loss across the band is the intra-subarray delay spread carried by $g_k(f)$. The array is split-free over $W$ if every subarray is split-free. The \ac{IBW} criterion is therefore applied to each subarray.
Substituting the local geometry $(L_k,\varphi_k,r_k)$ for $(L,\varphi_0,r_0)$ in \eqref{eqn-ula-ibw-angle} and \eqref{eqn-ula-ibw-range} yields
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ibwk}
\mathrm{IBW}_k^{\mathrm{ula}}=\min\!\left(\frac{0.886c}{L_k\sin\varphi_k},\ \frac{7\,c\,r_k}{L_k^{2}\cos^{2}\varphi_k}\right).
\end{equation}
The local coordinates $(\varphi_k,r_k)$ vary across subarrays, so the array \ac{IBW} is determined by the binding subarray,
$\displaystyle \mathrm{IBW}=\min_{1\le k\le K}\mathrm{IBW}_k^{\mathrm{ula}}$. Furthermore, a focal point at range $r_0$ lies in the local \ac{FF} of every subarray whenever $r_0\ge\RD/K^{2}$, where $\RD/K^{2}$ is the local Rayleigh distance of each subarray. Assuming a distance of $r_0=L$ yields the requirement $K\ge\sqrt{N}$, which guarantees local \ac{FF} operation for all focal points in
the radiative \ac{NF} of the full aperture. Under this partitioning, the radial beam split mechanism is suppressed within each subarray, and the \ac{IBW} is governed primarily by the angular beam split induced by the distinct local angles $\varphi_{k}$. In this case, the binding subarray is the one experiencing the largest local off-boresight angle, and therefore the smallest angular \ac{IBW}. This per-subarray variation is characteristic of the \ac{NF}. In the \ac{FF}, all subarrays observe the same local angle, so the array \ac{IBW} is equal to that of any subarray.
The array supports a bandwidth $W$ if every subarray satisfies the corresponding \ac{IBW} requirement. Accordingly, the minimum number of subarrays, equivalently the minimum number of \ac{RF} chains, is the smallest partition for which the \ac{IBW} of the binding subarray is no less than $W$
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Krule}
K_\mathrm{SC}^{\mathrm{ula}}=\min\Big\{K'\in\{1,\dots,N\}\ \Big|\ \min_{1\le k\le K'}\mathrm{IBW}_k^{\mathrm{ula}}\ge W\Big\}.
\end{equation}
The \ac{RF} chain count follows by evaluating \eqref{eq:Krule} over the candidate partitions. The stepwise procedure is summarized in Algorithm~\ref{alg:rfcount}.
Unlike \ac{FC} architectures, \ac{SC} hybrid arrays are susceptible to grating lobes if the subarray spacing $\Delta_s$ exceeds half a wavelength \cite{11523528,zhou2026near}. The grating lobes then bound the upper frequency offset $f_c+f_{\mathrm{high}}$, while the beam split bounds the lower one $f_c-f_{\mathrm{low}}$. Grating lobes can be suppressed through non-uniform subarray spacing or overlapped subarrays. Since grating lobes arise from spatial sampling rather than wideband dispersion, we exclude them from the scope of this work, which concerns \ac{NF} beam split analysis.
\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{RF Chain Count for the \acs{SC} Hybrid Array}
\label{alg:rfcount}
\renewcommand{\algorithmicrequire}{\textbf{Input:}}
\renewcommand{\algorithmicensure}{\textbf{Output:}}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require $N$, $L$, $W$, $(r_0,\varphi_0)$
\Ensure Minimum number of \acs{RF} chains $K_\mathrm{SC}^{\mathrm{ula}}$
\For{$K'=1,\dots,N $}
\State Partition the array into $K'$ subarrays
\For{$k=1,\ldots,K'$}
\State Compute $r_k$ and $\varphi_k$ from \eqref{eq:r_k} and \eqref{eq:phi_k}
\State Evaluate $\mathrm{IBW}_k^{\mathrm{ula}}$ from \eqref{eq:ibwk}
\EndFor
\If{$\displaystyle \min_{1\le k\le K'}\mathrm{IBW}_k^{\mathrm{ula}} \ge W$}
\State \Return $K_\mathrm{SC}^{\mathrm{ula}}\gets K'$
\EndIf
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}
\subsection{Fully-Connected Hybrid ULA}\label{sec-IVB} 
In contrast to the \ac{SC} architecture, each \ac{RF} chain in a \ac{FC} hybrid array is connected to all antenna elements via dedicated \acp{PS}. Consequently, this architecture achieves full aperture gain and yields more \ac{DoF} owing to the additional \acp{PS}. In this subsection, we derive the required \ac{RF} chain count to mitigate the \ac{NF} beam split while achieving the maximum gain. To this end, we analyze the eigenspectrum of the space-frequency correlation matrix, where its \ac{EDoF} dictates the required number of \ac{RF} chains.
\subsubsection{From Average Gain to the Correlation Matrix}
Consider a wideband \ac{NF} beamfocusing at $(\varphi_0,r_0)$ over a bandwidth $W$. We seek the fewest \ac{RF} chain count that holds the focus at $(\varphi_0,r_0)$ across $W$ while attaining the array gain $N$. The average beamforming gain over $W$ in \eqref{eq:gavg} is cast as
\begin{align}
g_{\mathrm{av}}
&\stackrel{(a)}{=}\frac{1}{W}\int_{-W/2}^{W/2}
\big\|(\mathbf W_a^{\mathsf{H}}\mathbf W_a)^{-1/2}\mathbf W_a^{\mathsf{H}}\mathbf a(f)\big\|^2\,df
\label{eq:gavg_b},
&\stackrel{(b)}{=}\operatorname{tr}\!\big(\mathbf P_{\mathbf W_a}\mathbf B\big),
\label{eq:gavg_c}
\end{align}
where $(a)$ follows by substituting the maximum beamforming gain in \eqref{eq:gf_bound}, and $(b)$ follows from the identity $\|\mathbf{v}\|^2=\operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{v}\mathbf{v}^{\mathsf{H}})$ \cite[Eq.~(17)]{petersen2008matrix}, together with the cyclic property of the trace, $\operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{AB})=\operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{BA})$ \cite[Eq.~(14)]{petersen2008matrix}. Here $\mathbf P_{\mathbf W_a}=\mathbf W_a(\mathbf W_a^{\mathsf{H}}\mathbf W_a)^{-1}\mathbf W_a^{\mathsf{H}}$ is the orthogonal projection matrix and $\mathbf B \in \mathbb{C}^ {N \times N}$ is the \textit{space-frequency correlation matrix}
\begin{equation}
\mathbf B=\frac{1}{W}\int_{-W/2}^{W/2}\mathbf a(f)\mathbf a^{\mathsf{H}}(f)\,df .
\label{eq:Bmatrix}
\end{equation}
To characterize the \ac{DoF}, we adopt a continuous-aperture model, which is an accurate analytical framework for extremely large-scale arrays \cite{10495774}. In this regime, the discrete antenna locations are replaced by continuous spatial variables $x, x' \in [-L/2, L/2]$. The exact propagation distance from the focal point to an arbitrary point $x$ on the aperture is
\begin{equation}
d(x)=\sqrt{r_0^2+x^2-2r_0x\sin\varphi_0}.
\label{eq:dexact_continuous}
\end{equation}
The discrete spatial correlation matrix $\mathbf B$ is thus generalized to a continuous spatial correlation kernel $B(x,x')$, obtained by integrating the outer product of the continuous array responses over the operating bandwidth
\begin{equation}
B(x,x')=\frac{1}{W}\int_{-W/2}^{W/2}
\exp\!\Big(-j\tfrac{2\pi(f_c+f)}{c}\,\delta(x,x')\Big)\,df,
\label{eq:B_kernel_integral}
\end{equation}
where $\delta(x,x')\triangleq d(x)-d(x')$ is the differential distance between the two aperture points. Evaluating the baseband integral yields
\begin{equation}
B(x,x')= \exp\!\Big(-j\tfrac{2\pi f_c}{c}\,\delta(x,x')\Big) \operatorname{sinc}\!\Big(\tfrac{W}{c}\delta(x,x')\Big).
\label{eq:B_sinc_continuous}
\end{equation}
The eigenspectrum of the spatial correlation operator is governed by the sinc kernel. Because the carrier-dependent exponential term constitutes a unitary phase transformation, it does not alter the eigenvalues of the integral operator \cite{10495774}.
\subsubsection{EDoF of Correlation Matrix}
The discrete spatial correlation matrix admits the eigendecomposition $\mathbf B=\sum_{\ell=1}^{N}\sigma_\ell\bm \nu_\ell\bm \nu_\ell^{{\mathsf{H}}}$, where $\sigma_1\ge\sigma_2\ge\cdots\ge\sigma_N\ge0$, and
$\{\sigma_\ell,\bm \nu_\ell\}_{\ell=1}^{N}$ are the eigenvalue--eigenvector pairs of $\mathbf B$. Substituting this decomposition into \eqref{eq:gavg_c} gives
\begin{equation}
g_{\mathrm{av}}
=\sum_{\ell=1}^{N}
\sigma_\ell
\big\|
\mathbf P_{\mathbf W_a}\bm \nu_\ell
\big\|^{2}.
\label{eq:gavg_eig}
\end{equation}
Since $\operatorname{rank}(\mathbf P_{\mathbf W_a})\le K$, at most $K$ eigenmodes can be captured by the analog beamformer. The average beamforming gain in \eqref{eq:gavg_eig} is maximized when the column space of $\mathbf W_a$ coincides with the eigenspace corresponding to the $K$ largest eigenvalues of $\mathbf B$ \cite{horn2012matrix}, yielding
\begin{equation}
\max_{\operatorname{rank}(\mathbf W_a)\le K}
g_{\mathrm{av}}
=
\sum_{\ell=1}^{K}
\sigma_\ell.
\label{eq:kyfan}
\end{equation}
Therefore, restoring $g_{\mathrm{av}}\rightarrow N$ requires the number of \ac{RF} chains $K$ to equal the number of dominant eigenvalues of $\mathbf B$, defined as the \ac{EDoF}. 
For large $N$, the eigenspectrum of the discrete matrix $\mathbf B$ asymptotically follows that of the continuous spatial kernel $B(x,x')$ defined in \eqref{eq:B_sinc_continuous}. The eigenvalues of this sinc operator exhibit a distinct step-like behavior \cite{7159085}, where the first $\alpha$ eigenvalues capture the entire trace, while the remaining eigenvalues rapidly decay toward zero. Consequently, we have
\begin{equation}
\sum_{\ell=1}^{\alpha}\sigma_\ell
\approx
\operatorname{tr}(\mathbf B)=N, \qquad \sigma_\ell\approx0 \ \ \text{for} \ \ \ell>\alpha,
\label{eq:knee}
\end{equation}
which implies that \ac{EDoF} equals $\alpha$. Therefore, allocating $K_\mathrm{FC}^{\mathrm{ula}}=\lceil\alpha\rceil$ \ac{RF} chains is sufficient to span the dominant eigenmodes and fully restore the array gain. Applying the eigenvalue counting theorem \cite{landau1975szego} to the continuous sinc kernel in \eqref{eq:B_sinc_continuous} shows that the \ac{EDoF} is given by the spatial bandwidth--delay product
\begin{equation}
\alpha = \frac{W}{c} \left( d_{\max} - d_{\min} \right),
\label{eq:alpha}
\end{equation}
where $d_{\max} = \max\limits_{|x| \le L/2} d(x)$ and $d_{\min} = \min\limits_{|x| \le L/2} d(x)$ denote the maximum and minimum path lengths from the focal point to the antenna array, respectively. Their difference, $d_{\max} - d_{\min}$, quantifies the maximum delay spread across the array that induces wideband spatial effects.
