\subsubsection{Closed-Form RF Chain Count}\label{sec-IVB3} 
The \ac{RF} chain count in \eqref{eq:alpha} scales with the maximum path-length difference across the aperture via the factor $W/c$, where the path-length difference is determined by the longest and shortest propagation paths from the \ac{UE} to the array. The edge path lengths follow by setting $x=\pm L/2$ in \eqref{eq:dexact_continuous}, as given by
\begin{equation}
d_{\pm}\triangleq
\sqrt{r_0^2+\frac{L^2}{4}\pm r_0L\sin\varphi_0}.
\label{eq:edge_dist}
\end{equation}
 The far edge always provides the longest path, so the maximum path length is $d_+$. The minimum path length instead depends on the orthogonal projection of the focal point onto the array line, whose foot lies at $x^{\star}=r_0\sin\varphi_0$, and two cases arise according to whether this projection falls on the aperture or beyond its edge, as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:ula_delay_cases}. The transition between the two cases occurs when the projection coincides with the array edge $x=L/2$, which gives the boundary
\begin{equation}
r_{\mathrm b}=\frac{L}{2\sin\varphi_0}.
\label{eq:rb}
\end{equation}
This boundary separates the two cases analyzed next.
\paragraph{Case A (Projection Outside the Aperture)}
This corresponds to $r_0\ge r_{\mathrm b}$, where the orthogonal projection falls outside the aperture. In this case, the minimum and maximum path lengths occur at the aperture edges, yielding
\begin{equation}
\alpha_{\mathrm A} = \frac{W}{c}\big(d_+-d_-\big).
\label{eq:alpha_A}
\end{equation}
\paragraph{Case B (Projection Inside the Aperture)}
This corresponds to $r_0<r_{\mathrm b}$, where the orthogonal projection lies within the aperture. The minimum path length is the perpendicular distance from the \ac{UE} to the array line, $d_{\min}
= r_0\cos\varphi_0$ while the maximum path length remains $d_+$. Therefore,
\begin{equation}
\alpha_{\mathrm B} = \frac{W}{c}\big(d_+-r_0\cos\varphi_0\big).
\label{eq:alpha_B}
\end{equation}
Combining both cases yields
\begin{equation}
\alpha(\varphi_0,r_0) = \frac{W}{c}
\begin{cases}
d_+-d_-,
&
r_0\ge\dfrac{L}{2\sin\varphi_0}, 
d_+-r_0\cos\varphi_0,
&
r_0<\dfrac{L}{2\sin\varphi_0},
\end{cases}
\label{eq:alpha_cases}
\end{equation}
and the minimum required number of \ac{RF} chains is given by $ K_\mathrm{FC}^{\mathrm{ula}} = \lceil\alpha\rceil.$
