We now illustrate the variation of $\alpha$ in \eqref{eq:alpha_cases} with a numerical example. We consider a \ac{ULA} of $N=256$ half-wavelength-spaced elements, which gives an aperture length $L\approx\unit[1.37]{m}$ at $f_c=\unit[28]{GHz}$. For illustration, we use a deliberately large operating bandwidth $W=\unit[28]{GHz}$. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:ibw_range_angle2}, for ${\varphi_0}<30^\circ$, $\alpha$ decreases with increasing $r_0$, whereas for ${\varphi_0}>30^\circ$ it increases with $r_0$. \textbf{In both panels of Fig. \ref{fig:ibw_range_angle2}, the variation becomes negligible beyond approximately $r_0\approx10L$}. 
To further explain the trends, we plot $\alpha$ as a function of distance and angle in Fig. \ref{fig:alpha_range_angle}, with Case A shaded orange and Case B shaded blue, separated by the boundary $r_b$ in \eqref{eq:rb}.
In Case A, $\alpha$ rises with distance and saturates near $r_0=10L$, with its largest value at endfire. Expanding \eqref{eq:edge_dist} for $r_0\gg L$ gives $d_\pm\to r_0\pm\tfrac{L}{2}\sin\varphi_0$, so the delay spread tends to $d_+-d_-\to L\sin\varphi_0$ and the count approaches the \ac{FF} value in \cite{10495774}
\begin{equation}
\alpha_{\mathrm A}\xrightarrow[r_0\gg L]{}\frac{WL\sin\varphi_0}{c}.
\label{eq:alpha_ff}
\end{equation}
At $\varphi_0=90^\circ$, it attains its maximum $WL/c\approx128$, which is the \ac{FF} endfire value.
At boresight, the angular beam split vanishes, and the count reduces to $\alpha=\tfrac{W}{c}\big(\sqrt{r_0^2+L^2/4}-r_0\big)$, which decays as $WL^2/(8cr_0)$ for $r_0\gg L$. The count therefore decreases monotonically with distance until it reaches one at the beam split boundary $r_\mathrm{sp}$ in \eqref{eq:nfbb}. For the very large bandwidth assumed in this example, the \ac{RF} chain count varies with distance even beyond $r_0=10L$. However, for nominal bandwidth values, the \ac{RF} chain count reduces to one well before the beam split boundary. In that case, the \ac{RF} chain count stays nearly constant beyond $10L$, as is the case for the other angles. 
In Case B, $\alpha$ exhibits a lower bound and two characteristic angular regions. For every angle, as $r_0\to0$, the focal point collapses onto the array. Consequently, $d_{\max}\to L/2$ and $d_{\min}\to0$, causing the count to approach an angle-independent \ac{NF} floor
\begin{equation}
\alpha_{\mathrm B}\xrightarrow[r_0\to0]{}\frac{WL}{2c}\approx64,
\label{eq:alpha_floor}
\end{equation}
which is half of the \ac{FF} endfire value. Furthermore, at $\varphi_0=30^\circ$, the \ac{NF} floor $WL/2c$ and the \ac{FF} value $WL\sin\varphi_0/c$ are identical. For $\varphi_0<30^\circ$ the \ac{FF} value lies below the \ac{NF} floor, so the count is higher near the array and lower far away. For $\varphi_0>30^\circ$ it lies above the \ac{NF} floor, so the count is lower near the array and higher far away.
The \ac{RF} chain count is bounded above by the \ac{FF} endfire value $\alpha_{\max}=WL/c\approx128$ and, within the focusing region, below by the angle-independent \ac{NF} floor $WL/2c\approx64$ reached as $r_0\to0$. Since the endpoint crossing at $\varphi_0=30^\circ$ places the \ac{FF} count above this floor for all $\varphi_0>30^\circ$, {a hybrid array dimensioned for \ac{FF} coverage over $|\varphi_0|\ge30^\circ$ already upper bounds the \ac{NF} demand across the focusing region, so no additional \ac{RF} chains are required for \ac{NF} operation}. 
The \ac{RF} chain count for the \ac{FC} architecture can be evaluated numerically from the space-frequency correlation matrix $\mathbf B$ in \eqref{eq:Bmatrix}. The number of dominant eigenvalues of $\mathbf B$, identified by the step behavior in \eqref{eq:knee}, gives $K_\mathrm{FC}^{\mathrm{ula}}=\lceil\alpha\rceil$, and the analog precoder $\mathbf W_a$ is formed from the associated leading eigenvectors $\{\bm \nu_\ell\}_{\ell=1}^{K_\mathrm{FC}^{\mathrm{ula}}}$. Arrays that implement the analog stage with \acp{PS} and no amplitude control restrict $\mathbf W_a$ to entries of constant modulus. The leading eigenvectors are mapped onto this constraint set by alternating projection between the dominant eigenspace and the constant-modulus manifold.
Applying the \ac{IBW} criterion developed in Section~\ref{subsec:ula_ibw} to the \ac{FC} architecture provides a coarse estimate of the required \ac{RF} chain count. Since each \ac{RF} chain connects to all $N$ antenna elements, the effective aperture equals the full array length $L$, allowing \eqref{eqn-ula-ibw-min} to apply directly. Consequently, supporting a total bandwidth $W$ requires $K_{\mathrm{FC}}^{\mathrm{ula}}=\lceil W/\mathrm{IBW}^{\mathrm{ula}}\rceil$ \ac{RF} chains. This expression broadly captures the scaling behavior across all operating regimes. However, minor deviations occur because the \ac{IBW} is defined for an allowable gain-loss threshold ($\rho_\epsilon$), whereas the $\alpha$-based chain count derived in \eqref{eq:alpha_cases} completely mitigates beam split to restore the full beamforming gain. 
\section{Extension to a Uniform Circular Array} \label{sec-V}
We now extend the preceding analysis to a \ac{UCA} of radius $R$ and aperture length $L=2R$. The position of each antenna element is represented as $(R, \psi_n)$, where $\psi_n = \frac{2\pi n}{N}$. The \ac{UE} is located at a distance $r$ from the array center, in the direction $(\theta,\varphi)$, where $\theta$ is the elevation angle \cite[Fig.~1]{11498324}. The path length from the \ac{UE} to the $\nth{n}$ antenna element is 
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
&d(\psi_n) = \sqrt{r^2+R^2-2Rr\sin\theta\cos(\psi_n-\varphi)}, \label{eq:uca_profile} 
 &{\overset{(a)}{\approx}} r-R\sin\theta\cos(\psi_n-\varphi) + \frac{R^2}{2r}\left(1-\sin^2\theta\cos^2(\psi_n-\varphi)\right), 
 \label{eq:uca_fresnel}
\end{align}
\end{subequations}\normalsize
where (a) follows from the Fresnel expansion \cite{10988573,11498324,10934779}. 
\subsection{Beam Split}
Following the same approach as in Section \ref{sec-III}, we evaluate the gain on the space--frequency plane using \eqref{eq:g_analog}. Substituting \eqref{eq:uca_fresnel} into \eqref{eq:g_analog} yields the per-element phase mismatch as
\begin{equation}\footnotesize
\begin{split}
\Xi_n(f)=
\underbrace{\frac{R^2}{2}\!\left(f_c\frac{\sin^2\theta_0\cos^2(\psi_n-\varphi_0)}{r_0}
-(f_c+f)\frac{\sin^2\theta\cos^2(\psi_n-\varphi)}{r}\right)}_{\text{curvature (distance) term}}  
-\underbrace{R\big(f_c\sin\theta_0\cos(\psi_n-\varphi_0)-(f_c+f)\sin\theta\cos(\psi_n-\varphi)\big)}_{\text{tilt (angle) term}} .
\label{eq:Xi_uca}
\end{split}
\end{equation}\normalsize
To evaluate the beam split, the tilt and curvature terms are set to zero, yielding the expressions for the angular and radial beam split, respectively.
\paragraph{Angular Beam Split}
Expanding $\cos(\psi_n-\varphi)=\cos\varphi\cos\psi_n+\sin\varphi\sin\psi_n$ in the tilt term of \eqref{eq:Xi_uca} and matching the $\cos\psi_n$ and $\sin\psi_n$ coefficients yields
\begin{equation}
\varphi=\varphi_0,
\qquad
\sin\theta=\frac{1}{1+\bar{f}}\,\sin\theta_0 .
\label{eq:uca_squint_angle}
\end{equation}
Mirroring the \ac{ULA} relation in \eqref{eq:squint_angle}, the elevation angle drifts with frequency. In contrast, the azimuth peak is frequency-invariant. The rotational symmetry of the \ac{UCA} renders an azimuth shift a relabeling of the elements, so no beam split occurs in azimuth.
\paragraph{Radial Beam Split}
Using $\cos^2(\psi_n-\varphi)=\tfrac12\big[1+\cos2(\varphi-\psi_n)\big]$ in the curvature term of \eqref{eq:Xi_uca} and matching the $\cos2\psi_n$ and $\sin2\psi_n$ coefficients yields
\begin{equation}
r=r_0 {(1+ \bar{f})}\,\frac{\sin^2\theta}{\sin^2\theta_0} .
\label{eq:uca_split_match}
\end{equation}
Substituting the split angle $\sin \theta$ from \eqref{eq:uca_squint_angle} simplifies it to
\begin{equation}
r=\frac{r_0}{1+\bar{f}}.
\label{eq:uca_split_range}
\end{equation}
In contrast to the \ac{ULA}, for $f>0$ the \ac{UCA} focal point drifts inward toward the array ($r<r_0$), and outward ($r>r_0$) for $f<0$. Furthermore, this radial split is independent of both the azimuth and elevation angles. 
Following the \ac{ULA} analysis and using the beamdepth of the \ac{UCA} at $f_c+f$, the radial beam split boundary can be shown to coincide with the \ac{EBRD} of the \ac{UCA}, given in \cite[Eq.~(10)]{11498324}. The derivation follows the same steps and is omitted for brevity.
