Next, we compare the \ac{UCA} with the \ac{ULA}. In the deep \ac{NF} $\alpha_{\mathrm{uca}}$ approaches zero, whereas $\alpha_{\mathrm{ula}}$ is bounded by the \ac{NF} floor $WL/2c$. In the distance domain, the \ac{UCA} mirrors the \ac{ULA}, with the curves saturating at $r_0\approx10L$.
\section{Numerical Results} \label{sec-VI}
In this section, we provide numerical results to validate the proposed \ac{RF} chain count expressions derived for the \ac{SC} and \ac{FC} hybrid architectures in Sections~\ref{sec-IV} and~\ref{sec-V}. To demonstrate the beam split mitigation, we evaluate the average beamforming gain, defined in \eqref{eq:gavg}, as a function of the number of \ac{RF} chains. Furthermore, we compare the \ac{RF} chain requirements between the \ac{SC} and \ac{FC} architectures across both geometries. Unless specified otherwise, the simulations assume a carrier frequency of $f_c = 28$~GHz and an operating bandwidth of $W = 3$~GHz. The array aperture is fixed at $L = 1.37$~m, which corresponds to $N = 256$ half-wavelength-spaced antennas for the \ac{ULA} and $N = 804$ antennas for the \ac{UCA}.
Fig.~\ref{fig:gain_vs_freq_IBW} illustrates the normalized beamforming gain $g(f)$ across the operating band for the \ac{SC} hybrid \ac{ULA}. The evaluation is performed at the focal point $(\varphi_0,r_0)=(90^{\circ},10L)$ for \ac{RF} chain counts $K\in\{1,4,16,32\}$, where Algorithm~\ref{alg:rfcount} returns $K_\mathrm{SC}^{\mathrm{ula}}=16$ for a bandwidth of $W=\unit[3]{GHz}$. As expected from the $1/L_k$ scaling in \eqref{eq:ibwk}, increasing $K$ reduces the physical aperture of each subarray, thereby widening its \ac{IBW}. For the algorithmically predicted count of $K=16$, the maximum in-band loss is $\unit[2.8]{dB}$, occurring at the band edges ($f=\pm\unit[1.5]{GHz}$). Because the threshold defined in \eqref{eq:ibwk} is enforced only at these band edges, the beamforming loss at all inner frequencies remains well below this limit. Moreover, only the binding subarray operates exactly at its \ac{IBW} limit. The remaining subarrays benefit from their specific local geometry $(L_k,\varphi_k,r_k)$ and maintain a wider \ac{IBW}. Consequently, the band-averaged beamforming gain exceeds the worst-case band-edge performance. This is illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:gain_vs_K}, which plots $g_{\mathrm{av}}$ versus $K$ for both array geometries. For instance, at $K=16$, the \ac{ULA} achieves an average gain of $g_{\mathrm{av}}=\unit[-1]{dB}$, outperforming the band-edge minimum from Fig.~\ref{fig:gain_vs_freq_IBW} by $\unit[1.8]{dB}$. When comparing geometries, the \ac{UCA} requires approximately $\pi$ times more subarrays than the \ac{ULA}. This occurs because dividing a ring into $K$ segments yields arc lengths of $\pi L/K$, compared to $L/K$ for a linear array, leaving each \ac{UCA} subarray with a larger aperture and a proportionally narrower \ac{IBW}. Finally, the threshold $\rho_\epsilon=0.95$ provides an additional \unit[1]{dB} of gain to achieve the full beamforming gain, but requires four times as many subarrays as the baseline $\rho_\epsilon=0.5$ case. 
