We compare the \ac{RF} chain requirements of the \ac{ULA} and \ac{UCA} geometries in Figs.~\ref{fig:RF_chains_ULA_vs_UCA_angle} and~\ref{fig:RF_chains_ULA_vs_UCA_distance}. Fig.~\ref{fig:RF_chains_ULA_vs_UCA_angle} evaluates the \ac{RF} chain count as a function of the azimuth angle at a fixed range. For the \ac{UCA}, this count remains constant across all azimuth angles, as it is determined only by the elevation angle. Conversely, for the \ac{ULA}, it depends on the azimuth angle. The \ac{RF} chain count reaches a minimum of $2$ chains at boresight (since range is fixed at $r_0=L$) and scales up to $WL/c=14$ at endfire direction. Fig.~\ref{fig:RF_chains_ULA_vs_UCA_distance} plots the required \ac{RF} chains against the normalized distance. Here, the \ac{RF} chain count for the \ac{UCA} rises monotonically from zero and approaches its \ac{FF} limit. In contrast, the \ac{ULA} count starts from a floor of $WL/2c=7$ in the deep \ac{NF}. While the \ac{UCA} requires fewer \ac{RF} chains than the \ac{ULA} at short propagation distances, ranges on the order of the aperture length $L$ are typically encountered only for very large array apertures, limiting the practical significance of this advantage.
Next, Fig.~\ref{fig:fc_vs_sc} compares the \ac{FC} and \ac{SC} architectures in terms of required \ac{RF} chain counts for both array geometries as a function of the angle, evaluated at a fixed distance of $10L$. For a fair comparison, the elevation angle of the \ac{UCA} is set equal to the azimuth angle of the \ac{ULA}. The elevation angle plays the same role in \ac{UCA} as the azimuth angle in \ac{ULA}. The \ac{FC} \ac{RF} chain counts of the two geometries coincide over most of the angular sweep and are bounded by $WL/c$. Conversely, the \ac{SC} counts lie above their \ac{FC} counterparts at every angle. Because the block-diagonal precoder in \ac{SC} systems confines each \ac{RF} chain to a disjoint subset of elements, it cannot span the dominant eigenmodes of $\mathbf{B}$. Instead, it must shrink the physical size of every subarray until its individual \ac{IBW} exceeds $W$. This hardware penalty is modest for the \ac{ULA} but pronounced for the \ac{UCA}, which corroborates the arc-length penalty previously observed in Fig.~\ref{fig:gain_vs_K}. Therefore, to mitigate the beam split, \ac{FC} architectures require fewer \ac{RF} chains than \ac{SC} configurations. However, this reduction in \ac{RF} chains comes at the cost of an increased phase shifter network. Specifically, the \ac{FC} architecture requires ($K \times N$) \acp{PS}, whereas the \ac{SC} architecture requires only $N$ \cite{10495774}. In addition, the larger number of \acp{PS} and the associated RF combining network introduce higher insertion losses, which can offset the performance gains of the \ac{FC} architecture, particularly in wideband beamforming scenarios. 
For a fixed aperture length, a \ac{UCA} accommodates a greater number of antenna elements than a \ac{ULA}. Consequently, in \ac{FC} architectures, the ratio of \ac{RF} chains to antenna elements is lower for the \ac{UCA} than for the \ac{ULA}. Conversely, in \ac{SC} architectures, this normalized \ac{RF} chain count remains approximately equivalent across both array geometries.
