\section{Conclusion} \label{sec-VII}
In this paper, we derived the \ac{RF} chain count required to mitigate the beam split in wideband \ac{NF} hybrid arrays. We characterized the spatial boundary beyond which the radial beam split vanishes and used the \ac{NF} \ac{IBW} to obtain the \ac{RF} chain count for \ac{SC} architectures. For \ac{FC} architectures, the \ac{RF} chain requirement was determined via the eigenspectrum of the space-frequency correlation matrix. We demonstrated that \ac{FC} arrays require fewer \ac{RF} chains than \ac{SC} arrays, trading \ac{RF} chain count for increased phase shifter complexity. Moreover, for the \ac{FC} architecture, \acp{UCA} require fewer \ac{RF} chains than \acp{ULA} in the deep \ac{NF}. This analytical framework provides a foundation for channel estimation via wideband \ac{NF} codebooks constructed from the eigenvectors of the space-frequency correlation matrix \cite{11503877}. Future work will expand this analysis to multipath channels and planar array geometries \cite{11428208}. Additionally, because uniform arc arrays may entail a reduced time-delay spread due to their geometric curvature \cite{10843386}, comparing their wideband performance against \acp{ULA} warrants further investigation.
