\subsection{Implementation of Proposed Approach}
The proposed approach used 
a pre-trained autoencoder to facilitate bound computation. In our experiments, we used 
the variational autoencoder from 
Stable Diffusion v1, a latent diffusion modeling framework \cite{rombach2022highresolution}. In particular, we used the model architecture designed for RGB images of size $3 \times 512 \times 512$, with a latent space of size 
$4 \times 64 \times 64$. To accommodate the single-channel $1024 \times 1024$ data, 
we 
modified the autoencoder by incorporating a fixed down-sampling and broadcasting layer at the start of the encoder and a fixed up-sampling and averaging layer at the end of the decoder. 
Because the 
values of $\mu_a$ 
span several orders of magnitude, which can be difficult for the autoencoder to handle, we prepended 
a pointwise logarithmic transformation to the encoder 
and shifted and scaled the transformed 
data 
into $[-1, 1]^D$. The inverse of this transformation was 
appended to the decoder. Notably, no fine-tuning was performed. Figure \ref{fig:recon_results} shows uncurated $\bsmu_a$ maps 
from our dataset 
together with their corresponding autoencoder reconstructions. 
The reconstructions exhibit high visual fidelity without noticeable 
distortions, and 
the average relative squared reconstruction error 
over the entire dataset was $7.67\
\begin{figure}
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Figures/reconlatentfigure.png}
 \caption{Uncurated $\bsmu_a$ maps from our dataset (top), 
 the corresponding reconstructions obtained using the autoencoder (middle), and the associated 
 error maps (bottom). 
 Despite not being trained or fine-tuned on our dataset, the autoencoder produced accurate reconstructions without noticeable visual distortions.} 
 \label{fig:recon_results}
\end{figure}
We implemented both the original and proposed weighted SSM objective functions in the latent space using \eqref{eq:ssm_empirical_weighted} with $\mathbf{W}=\mathbf{I}$ and $\mathbf{W}=\mathbf{I}+\mathbf{L}$, respectively. The two-dimensional Laplacian operator $\mathbf{L}$ was implemented in Fourier space with symmetric mirror padding 
to prevent aliasing. 
We used the same network architecture and training procedure 
for both the original and weighted SSM objectives. The network $\eta_{\bstheta}$ in \eqref{eq:energy_net_lsr_param} was implemented as 
a four-layer convolutional neural network with 
Softplus activations 
and a kernel size of $3$. The three hidden layers contained 
$16$, $32$, and $16$ channels, respectively, resulting in a 
total of 
$10{,}437$ model parameters. The SSM objective in \eqref{eq:ssm_empirical_weighted} was implemented with $M = 10$. 
We optimized the network weights using the AdamW optimizer \cite{loshchilov2017decoupled} with a weight decay of $10^{-4}$ and a learning rate of $2 \times 10^{-4}$. Gradient clipping and exponential moving averages of the network weights were incorporated for stability. The 
networks trained 
with the original and weighted SSM objectives 
were each optimized 
for $450{,}000$ 
iterations. 
