To isolate the effect 
of the learned prior on the Bayesian CRB, we first examine the estimates of $\bsJ^P_{\bsz}$ 
obtained using both the original and proposed SSM objectives. These are mapped to image space using the change-of-variables formula in \eqref{eq:image_space_cov_estimate}. 
Specifically, we computed estimates of  
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:jp_pushforward}
\bsV^P_{\bsm} \triangleq \bsC \, \bsV^{P}_{\bsz} \, \bsC^T, \quad \bsV^P_{\bsz} \triangleq \left( \bsJ_{\bsz}^P \right)^{-1}.
\end{equation}
Figure \ref{fig:jp_estimates} shows the estimates of $\bsV^P_{\bsm} $ 
obtained using 
the original and proposed SSM objectives, together with the pointwise variance of the samples in the dataset $\{ \bsm_i \}_{i=1}^{N}$. Three observations are noteworthy. 
First, both 
bound estimates exhibited 
spatial structure similar to that of the sample pointwise variance, indicating 
that the bound estimates captured 
features present in the training distribution. 
Second, both bound estimates were 
several orders of magnitude smaller than the pointwise sample variance. This is 
expected because the pointwise variance of the samples provides an upper bound on the diagonal of $\bsV^P_{\bsm}$ 
that is attained only when the $\bsm$ distribution is Gaussian (see, e.g., \cite{bell_2013_detection}), while the distribution of the training data is highly non-Gaussian. Finally, although 
the estimates obtained with the two different objectives exhibited 
similar global structure, the estimate obtained using the original SSM objective contained 
high-frequency artifacts that were absent from 
the estimate obtained using the proposed objective. This observation is 
consistent with the stronger penalty 
that the proposed objective places on 
high-frequency components of the error in the learned score. 
As with the prior term, we  
examined the impact of the data term $\bsJ^D_{\bsz}$ by computing  
estimates of
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:jd_pushforward}
\bsV_{\bsm}^D \triangleq \bsC \, \bsV_{\bsz}^D \, \bsC^T, \quad \bsV_{\bsz}^D \triangleq \left ( \bsJ_{\bsz}^D \right)^{-1}. 
\end{equation}
Here, $\bsV_{\bsz}^D$ 
is the inverse of the expected Fisher information associated with 
the likelihood 
and corresponds to the latent-space Bayesian CRB under  
an uninformative prior. The matrix $\bsV_{\bsm}^D$ is the corresponding image-space bound obtained using the change-of-variables formula. Figure \ref{fig:jd_bound_estimates} shows the diagonals of both the estimated uninformative-prior Bayesian CRB $\bsV_{\bsm}^D$  
and the learned-prior Bayesian CRB $\bsV_{\bsm}$. 
For the latter, the prior score was learned using the proposed SSM objective.
As can be seen, the spatial structure of the bounds under the uninformative prior was 
consistent with that of 
the fluence maps in Fig. \ref{fig:fluence_figure}. In particular, both illumination 
schemes led 
to large bound values 
near the center of the domain, where signal attenuation was 
strongest. The 
relative performance 
of the two schemes, as shown in the final 
column of Fig.~\ref{fig:jd_bound_estimates}, was 
consistent with 
the contiguous design concentrating 
its illuminations in four distinct regions. Incorporation of the prior term $\bsJ_{\bsz}^P$ altered 
both the spatial structure of the bounds and the relative strengths of the two designs. In particular, the Bayesian CRB under the data-informed prior was dominated by the prior term near 
the center of the domain 
because 
signal attenuation was severe and the training samples exhibited 
relatively low variance in this region. 
Table \ref{tab:metric_results} reports 
the trace and log-determinant 
of the estimates of the latent-space bounds $\bsV_{\bsz}^D$ and $\bsV_{\bsz}$, together with the trace of 
their counterparts in the original parameter space. Note that the trace and log-determinant correspond to the A-optimal and D-optimal design criteria, respectively, and are commonly used metrics in experimental design \cite{pukelsheim_1993_optimal}. The results are consistent with 
the findings in Fig. \ref{fig:jd_bound_estimates} and further demonstrate that the learned prior substantially affects 
the structure of the bound. In particular, the incorporation of the data-informed prior changed 
the ranking 
of the two design schemes when 
the trace of the bound in parameter space was used as the design criterion. 
This finding suggests that the prior can play an important role in data-informed experimental design. 
\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:discuss_conclude}
In this work, we introduced a new framework to facilitate 
data-driven Bayesian CRB estimation for high-dimensional imaging systems. The framework uses a pre-trained variational autoencoder to reformulate the bound estimation problem in the autoencoder latent space, which dramatically decreases the dimensionality of the bound estimation problem. A novel SM 
variant, 
SSM 
in Bochner spaces, was 
also introduced to facilitate learning the score of the latent-space prior distribution. By formulating the SSM objective within a Bochner space that accounts for the spatial regularity of the field, we effectively penalized discrepancies in the spatial gradients of the score. The results demonstrated that this approach suppressed 
high-frequency artifacts, leading to significantly more stable downstream Bayesian CRB estimates compared with the original SSM 
objective. Furthermore, the data-informed prior was 
shown to play a significant role in the structure of the resulting bound, emphasizing the importance of accurately 
capturing the prior distribution when using the bound for downstream tasks such as optimal experimental design 
and estimator benchmarking. 
This work represents a promising step toward making the Bayesian CRB a practical tool for the design and analysis of high-dimensional imaging systems. However, several 
limitations remain 
to be addressed to realize this potential. First, the proposed approach was evaluated 
using a highly 
stylized model of the underlying imaging physics. In particular, the nonlinear PDE-based models typically used to model 
light propagation in qPACT were sidestepped, leading to a 
simple linear-Gaussian likelihood function. We also used a two-dimensional model of the imaging system, whereas most 
qPACT systems used in practice are inherently three-dimensional. Moreover, we neglected 
the 
effects of unknown tissue parameters that are not of direct clinical interest, 
such as optical scattering, speed-of-sound heterogeneity, and acoustic attenuation. These parameters are known 
to play a significant role in qPACT image formation \cite{cox2009challenges}. New techniques will likely be required to address the additional computational challenges associated with these complications. Second, rigorous comparisons of the bound estimates obtained from 
the proposed framework with the error maps of estimators for 
the corresponding inverse problem are needed. Examining the relationship 
between the bound and the error maps would help assess 
the validity of the bound estimate as a meaningful estimator benchmark and as a guide for experimental design. 
The Bochner-space SSM objective introduced here also warrants further investigation as a stand-alone technique for score learning. In 
this work, we avoided directly formulating the Bochner-space SSM objective in the infinite-dimensional function space setting that motivated its development. 
However, there has been substantial recent work on the development of infinite-dimensional SM 
approaches in the context of denoising diffusion models \cite{lim2023score, pidstrigach2023infinite}. A formal comparison between Bochner-space SSM and Sobolev-norm denoising SM 
could provide key insights into the trade-offs between computational efficiency and the preservation of function-space structure.
