To address these challenges, we propose a
Topology-informed Prompt-conditioned Universal Segmentation (\textbf{TPUS}) framework for unified multi-structure segmentation across uterine ultrasound and MRI. 
As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig_pipeline}, TPUS is designed to jointly learn representations across heterogeneous datasets while adapting the segmentation process to dataset-specific label spaces. 
Specifically, we introduce task-aware class prompts to explicitly condition the model on different segmentation targets, enabling flexible prediction across heterogeneous label definitions. 
A dynamic convolutional adaptation module further generates task-specific output responses, allowing the representation to be effectively adapted to different modalities and segmentation tasks. 
To preserve anatomical consistency, we incorporate a topology-informed loss that encourages structurally plausible predictions. 
In addition, we develop a graph-based multi-dataset backbone comprising modality-specific stems and a modality-shared graph-based encoder-decoder, enabling modality-sensitive input adaptation and joint representation learning across heterogeneous uterine segmentation tasks.
These components enable TPUS to jointly learn from ultrasound and MRI while mitigating negative transfer and improving multi-structure uterine segmentation.
Experiments on uterine ultrasound and T2-weighted MRI datasets demonstrate that the proposed TPUS consistently outperforms several generic and universal segmentation methods. 
\section{METHODOLOGY}
\label{sec:method}
{The proposed TPUS addresses cross-modality appearance variation and dataset-specific label spaces through modality-sensitive input adaptation, joint representation learning, prompt-conditioned prediction, and topology-informed deep supervision (Fig.~\ref{fig_pipeline}).} 
\subsection{Graph-Based Multi-Dataset Backbone}
The graph-based multi-dataset backbone comprises modality-specific stems and a modality-shared graph-based encoder-decoder. 
Let $\mathcal{D}=\{\mathcal{D}^{(d)}\}_{d=1}^{K}$ denote $K$ datasets, where dataset $d$ provides an image $\mathbf{x}^{(d)}$ and a label map $\mathbf{y}^{(d)}$ defined over its own class set $\mathcal{C}_d$.
TPUS follows the MultiTalent multi-dataset paradigm~\cite{ulrich2023multitalent}, while separating modality-sensitive input processing from shared anatomical representation learning.
As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig_pipeline}(a), each dataset has an independent stem $\mathcal{S}_d$ comprising a $3\!\times\!3\!\times\!3$ convolution, instance normalization, and LeakyReLU that maps its one-channel volume to 32 channels:
\begin{equation}
 \mathbf{z}^{(d)}_0=\mathcal{S}_d\!\left(\mathbf{x}^{(d)}\right).
 \label{eq:stem}
\end{equation}
At each iteration, one sampled volume is routed through its corresponding stem. With the reported batch size of one, the concatenation operator in Fig.~\ref{fig_pipeline}(a) simply forwards the selected stem output rather than pairing US and MRI volumes. The output then enters the modality-shared six-level residual U-shaped encoder-decoder in Fig.~\ref{fig_pipeline}(b), whose encoder widths are 32, 64, 128, 256, 320, and 320 channels and whose decoder widths are 320, 256, 128, 64, and 32 channels. Resolution-matched skip connections link the two paths; all parameters after the stems are shared.
Within the modality-shared graph-based encoder-decoder, we adapt the Pool graph neural network (GNN) and Swin GNN~\cite{shi2023nextou} into several topology blocks ({TopoBlocks}) deployed at encoder levels $\mathrm{E}_2$--$\mathrm{E}_5$ and decoder levels $\mathrm{D}_0$--$\mathrm{D}_2$; high-resolution levels $\mathrm{E}_0$--$\mathrm{E}_1$ and $\mathrm{D}_3$--$\mathrm{D}_4$ retain convolutional residual blocks. 
The TopoBlock shown in the bottom right of Fig.~\ref{fig_pipeline} applies graph reasoning and feed-forward network (FFN) refinement residually to feature $\mathbf{F}$:
\begin{align}
 \widehat{\mathbf{F}}_{p} &= \mathcal{G}_{p}(\mathbf{F})+\mathbf{F}, &
 \mathbf{F}_{p} &= \operatorname{FFN}(\widehat{\mathbf{F}}_{p})+\widehat{\mathbf{F}}_{p}, \nonumber
 \widehat{\mathbf{F}}_{w} &= \mathcal{G}_{w}(\mathbf{F}_{p})+\mathbf{F}_{p}, &
 \mathbf{F}' &= \operatorname{FFN}(\widehat{\mathbf{F}}_{w})+\widehat{\mathbf{F}}_{w},
 \label{eq:gnn}
\end{align}
where $\mathcal{G}_{p}$ denotes Pool GNN, which builds a dynamic $k$-nearest-neighbor graph after max pooling and restores resolution by max unpooling, and $\mathcal{G}_{w}$ denotes Swin GNN, which builds dynamic graphs within shifted 3D windows. Both use max-relative graph convolution to complement the backbone's local convolutional bias.
\subsection{Prompt-Conditioned Dynamic Head}
Figure~\ref{fig_pipeline}(c) shows the class-text, image-context, and prediction paths of the dynamic head. For each class $c\in\mathcal{C}_d$, including background, we form a task-aware prompt, such as ``uterus in a gynecologic ultrasound image'' or ``uterine myoma in a sagittal T2 pelvic MRI image.'' A frozen CLIP ViT-B/32 text encoder~\cite{radford2021learning} yields an embedding $\mathbf{e}_{d,c}\in\mathbb{R}^{512}$. Then TPUS combines class semantics with image context to generate the classifier. The $\mathrm{E}_5$ bottleneck feature $\mathbf{B}$ and class embedding are projected to 256 dimensions:
\begin{align}
 \mathbf{i} &= \operatorname{Conv}\!\left(\operatorname{GAP}
 \left(\operatorname{ReLU}(\operatorname{GN}(\mathbf{B}))\right)\right), \nonumber
 \mathbf{t}_{d,c} &= \operatorname{ReLU}(\mathbf{W}_{t}\mathbf{e}_{d,c}+\mathbf{b}_{t}), \nonumber
 \boldsymbol{\theta}_{d,c} &= \mathcal{H}\!\left([\mathbf{i}\,\|\,\mathbf{t}_{d,c}]\right),
 \label{eq:conditioning}
\end{align}
where $[\cdot\|\cdot]$ denotes channel-wise concatenation at the circled C in Fig.~\ref{fig_pipeline}(c),  the shared $1\!\times\!1\!\times\!1$ controller $\mathcal{H}$ maps the 512-D condition to $\boldsymbol{\theta}_{d,c}\in\mathbb{R}^{153}$, GAP denotes global average pooling, ReLU represents the rectified linear unit, and GN represents group normalization. 
In the prediction path, the final $\mathrm{D}_4$ decoder feature $\mathbf{F}_{4}\in\mathbb{R}^{32\times D\times H\times W}$ is transformed into an eight-channel map:
\begin{equation*}
 \mathbf{Q}=\operatorname{Conv}
 \!\left(
 \operatorname{ReLU}(\operatorname{GN}(\mathbf{F}_{4}))\right). 
\end{equation*}
The dynamic convolution (DynConv) block in Fig.~\ref{fig_pipeline}(c) comprises three class-wise dynamic convolutions parameterized by $\boldsymbol{\theta}_{d,c}$:
\begin{equation}
 \begin{aligned}
 \mathbf{q}^{1}_{d,c} &= \sigma(\mathbf{W}^{1}_{d,c}*\mathbf{Q}+\mathbf{b}^{1}_{d,c}),
 \mathbf{q}^{2}_{d,c} &= \sigma(\mathbf{W}^{2}_{d,c}*\mathbf{q}^{1}_{d,c}+\mathbf{b}^{2}_{d,c}),
 \mathbf{o}_{d,c} &= \mathbf{W}^{3}_{d,c}*\mathbf{q}^{2}_{d,c}+\mathbf{b}^{3}_{d,c},
 \end{aligned}
 \label{eq:dynamic_head}
\end{equation}
where $*$ is a $1\!\times\!1\!\times\!1$ convolution, $\sigma$ is ReLU, and the channel dimensions are $8\!\rightarrow\!8\!\rightarrow\!8\!\rightarrow\!1$.
Each class therefore requires $(8^2+8)+(8^2+8)+(8+1)=153$ generated parameters. The controller is shared across datasets and classes, but the resulting filters depend on both image and class. CLIP embeddings remain fixed, whereas their projection and the controller are trainable. This head replaces the fixed $\mathrm{D}_4$ classifier and produces the native multiclass logits; softmax yields the corresponding prediction. Unlike previous CLIP-driven dynamic convolution~\cite{liu2023clipdriven}, which predicts a fixed foreground vocabulary using independent sigmoid outputs, TPUS uses task-aware class prompts to generate background-inclusive softmax logits in each dataset's native label space.
\subsection{Topology-Informed Deep Supervision}
We apply deep supervision to outputs at decoder $\mathrm{D}_s$, $s\in\{1,2,3,4\}$.
Let $\mathbf{o}^{s}$ and $\mathbf{y}^{s}$ denote the logits and corresponding target at $\mathrm{D}_s$.
We define pairwise exclusion relations among a dataset's foreground labels. Unlike previous topology-informed losses~\cite{shi2023nextou}, which require manually specified relations for a fixed label space, TPUS automatically derives pairwise exclusions from each dataset's native foreground labels. Hard predictions $\mathbf{P}^{s}$ are obtained by taking the class-wise argmax of the softmax probabilities. A $3\!\times\!3\!\times\!3$ kernel is then applied to $\mathbf{P}^{s}$ to identify violations within each voxel's 26-connected neighborhood, yielding a binary critical-voxel mask $\mathbf{V}^{s}$. For batch size $N$ and voxel domain $\Omega_s$, the topological interaction (TI) term applies cross-entropy only at these voxels:
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal{L}^{s}_{\mathrm{T}}=
 \frac{1}{N}\sum\nolimits_{n=1}^{N}\sum\nolimits_{v\in\Omega_s}
 V^{s}_{n,v}\,\operatorname{CE}(\mathbf{o}^{s}_{n,v},\mathbf{y}^{s}_{n,v}).
 \label{eq:ti_loss}
\end{equation}
For a single foreground class, the exclusion set is empty and $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{T}}^s=0$; the four UMD foreground classes yield six exclusion pairs. The total loss is formulated as follows: 
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal{L} =\sum\nolimits_{s=1}^{4}w_s\mathcal{L}^s
 =\sum\nolimits_{s=1}^{4}w_s\left(
 \mathcal{L}^{s}_{\mathrm{Dice}}+\mathcal{L}^{s}_{\mathrm{CE}}+
 \lambda_{\mathrm{T}}\mathcal{L}^{s}_{\mathrm{T}}\right),


 \label{eq:overall_loss}
\end{equation}
where $\lambda_{\mathrm{T}}$ is empirically set as $10^{-6}$. 
We use normalized deep-supervision weights $w_s=2^{s-1}/15$ for $\mathrm{D}_s$ ($s\in\{1,2,3,4\}$). Only the full-resolution dynamic output at $\mathrm{D}_4$ is used at inference.
