\begin{abstract}
The beamforming problem of intelligent reflecting surface (IRS) has been extensively considered from an optimization perspective assuming that channel state information (CSI) is available. However, the reality is that the existing prototypes seldom follow this model-based approach because channel estimation is technically difficult and costly for the network protocols and hardware to date. A recent trend is to perform beamforming blindly without channel knowledge. This work looks at blind beamforming from a reinforcement learning point of view. We first show that the existing blind beamforming method boils down to a special case of the greedy algorithm in the reinforcement learning context. We analyze the resulting cumulative regret, and further propose an upper approximation to facilitate the optimization of the exploration probability. Moreover, we show that a gradient sampling scheme can improve the efficiency of reinforcement learning as compared to the uniform sampling scheme adopted in the existing blind beamforming method. We further verify the convergence of the proposed gradient sampling scheme by showing that it is a stochastic approximation to gradient ascent. Finally, we physically implement the proposed method in a real-world prototype system. Our field test results show that, as compared to the existing blind beamforming method, 
the proposed gradient sampling boosts the average signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) by more than 5 dB with 1000 samples.
\end{abstract}
\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Intelligent reflecting surface (IRS), blind beamforming, reinforcement learning.
\end{IEEEkeywords}
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
\IEEEPARstart{I}{ntelligent} reflecting surface (IRS) \cite{wu2021intelligent,gong2020toward}, a.k.a. reconfigurable intelligent surface (RIS) \cite{huang2019reconfigurable,liu2021reconfigurable}, is an emerging wireless technology that harnesses the signal reflection paths to enhance wireless transmission. An IRS consists of an array of reflective elements (REs)---which induce phase shifts into their respective incident signals. A major use of the IRS is to coordinate phase shifts across its REs, i.e., \emph{passive beamforming}, in order to maximize the received signal power at the target position. The existing approaches to passive beamforming typically entail channel state information (CSI), whereas some recent works \cite{Arun2020RFocus,ren2022configuring,Abdallah2024MADRL,Bin2026adaptive,Wang2025BlindInterference} suggest optimizing phase shifts blindly without channel acquisition. We further develop this new idea by means of reinforcement learning: 
\begin{itemize}
 \item We show that the blind beamforming method in \cite{Arun2020RFocus, ren2022configuring} is equivalent to an $\epsilon$-greedy algorithm for the multi-armed bandit problem \cite{cesabianchi1998finite,auer2002finite,vermorel2005multi}. We analyze the resulting cumulative regret, and further propose an upper approximation to make the optimization of the exploration probability easier.
 \item We improve the efficiency of blind beamforming by replacing uniform samples with gradient samples. Specifically, we show that gradient samples enable faster convergence, allowing better performance to be achieved with fewer samples. Moreover, we demonstrate the advantage of the proposed gradient sampling scheme in field tests.
\end{itemize}
In the literature, passive beamforming has been considered extensively from a model-based optimization perspective: it first estimates the channels for all the propagation paths to formulate the problem model explicitly, and then optimizes the phase shifts based on the problem model. When CSI is available, the phase shift optimization for IRS has been well studied. Many existing works assume that phase shifts can be arbitrarily chosen within the interval $[0,2\pi)$, so the phase shifting problem is a continuous optimization problem. As a result, the passive beamforming problem of IRS can be addressed by the standard methods, e.g., semidefinite relaxation (SDR)
\cite{luo2010semidefinite, zhou2020robust, zeng2020sum, xie2020max, huang2020decentralized} and fractional programming (FP) \cite{shen2018fractional_p1, feng2020physical,zhu2020power,shafique2020optimization,zhang2022active,zhang2021joint}.
However, in practice, the choice of phase shift is typically limited to a discrete set due to the hardware constraint, e.g., each RE takes on its phase shift from the binary set $\{0,\pi\}$ in the 1-bit phase shifting case. To account for the discrete constraint, a common heuristic \cite{Feng2021RFlens,wu2020beamforming,li2020joint,you2020channel} is to relax the problem and then round the continuous solution to the discrete set; the optimal solutions are known only for the single-antenna case \cite{zhang2022optimal,ren2023optimal}.
But estimating channels for an IRS-assisted network is by no means trivial; a variety of sophisticated strategies have been proposed. In \cite{mishra2019channel, yang2020intelligent,lin2020reconfigurable}, the channel estimation is conducted assuming that each RE can be turned off, i.e., every {\footnotesize{OFF}} RE absorbs all signals falling on it.
Another line of work \cite{zheng2019intelligent,jensen2020optimal,de2021channel} requires introducing extra pilots according to the discrete Fourier transform (DFT) matrix. Assuming a certain type of sparse model for channels, \cite{chen2023channel} suggests a two-step method that first estimates the common column of AoD steering vectors and then solves the sparse matrix recovery problem. In particular, we mention that all the above methods entail full cooperation between the IRS and the base station.
However, it is difficult to carry out these channel estimation methods in real-world networks because of the following two practical issues:
\begin{enumerate}[i.]
 \item Each reflected channel alone can be easily overwhelmed by the much stronger direct channel, and hence is difficult to measure.
 \item The existing channel estimation methods for IRS are incompatible with the current networking protocols and hardware.
\end{enumerate}
As such, even though the model-driven approach has been extensively studied in the literature to date, the existing works \cite{Arun2020RFocus, ren2022configuring, pei2021ris, staat2022irshield} that pursue prototype implementations of IRS rarely consider channel estimation.
Due to the difficulties and high cost of channel estimation, there has been an increasing interest in configuring IRS without CSI. For instance, \cite{psomas2021low} proposes a distributed ascent algorithm for the continuous IRS beamforming based on the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) feedback, and \cite{cai2023toward} proposes an IRS-aided joint index keying $M$-ary differential chaos shift keying system where the receiver can retrieve information bits by performing non-coherent correlation demodulation without CSI. The commonly used beam training method \cite{you2020fast,wang2022fast,wang2021jointbeam} constitutes another line of endeavors to perform blind beamforming, which simply tries out a sequence of random beamformers and then picks the best one. Although the core idea of beam training is fairly simple, the performance of this method is limited by the size of random samples. Another existing idea is to use the neural network to learn the direct mapping from the received pilot signals to the phase shift decision \cite{2022Xuwy,2020HuangcwJSAC,2021JiangtJSAC}. However, the parameter tuning of the neural network can be quite tricky.
