Our work is most closely related to the blind beamforming method in \cite{Arun2020RFocus, ren2022configuring} based on statistics. Specifically, \cite{ren2022configuring} initiates the idea of using the conditional sample mean (CSM) to configure the IRS for an isolated point-to-point link. Its main idea follows: Choose phase shifts for all REs at random, then use the average performance conditioned on a particular choice of phase shift for the $n$th RE to characterize the goodness of this phase shift decision, and finally choose a phase shift for each RE $n$ to maximize the corresponding conditional average performance. More recently, the CSM algorithm has been extended for multiple IRSs \cite{Xu2024Coordinating}, for multiple users \cite{xu2024blind}, and for the Non-line-of-sight (NLoS) transmission \cite{lai2025adaptive}. While \cite{Arun2020RFocus, ren2022configuring} give the statistical motivation behind blind beamforming, this work shows that blind beamforming can be interpreted as an $\epsilon$-greedy algorithm \cite{watkins1989learning} for solving the multi-armed bandit problem in the reinforcement learning field; the key step is to figure out ``cumulative regret'' in our problem case. Following this line, we further extend the gradient bandit method \cite{sutton2018reinforcement} to improve the efficiency of blind beamforming. Intuitively, rather than trying out new samples uniformly, we propose trying out those more ``promising'' samples with higher priorities so as to attain the optimum more quickly. More importantly, we show that the proposed gradient sampling scheme is a stochastic approximation to the gradient ascent, so its convergence is guaranteed. Furthermore, we build a prototype system to evaluate this gradient sampling scheme in a real-world testbed; the field test results show that it can outperform the existing blind beamforming method significantly.
