\section{System Model}
\label{sec:sys_model}
Consider an IRS-assisted wireless network as shown in Fig. \ref{fig:system}. Assume that the transmitter and receiver have one antenna each, and that the IRS consists of $N$ REs in total. Every RE can induce a reflected path from the transmitter to the receiver. Denote by $h_0\in\mathbb C$ the direct channel from the transmitter to the receiver, $h_{n,t}\in\mathbb C$ the channel from the transmitter to RE $n$, and $h_{n,r}\in\mathbb C$ the channel from RE $n$ to the receiver, for $n=1,\ldots,N$.
Each cascaded reflected channel $h_n$ can then be factored as
\begin{equation}
 h_n = h_{n,r}\times h_{n,t}.
\end{equation}
Note that we do not assume any channel model for both the direct and reflected channels. In this work, we assume that the channels are unknown \emph{a priori}.
\begin{figure}[t]
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{fig/system_model.pdf}
 \caption{The IRS-assisted wireless network.}
 \label{fig:system}
\end{figure}
Moreover, each RE $n$ can induce a phase shift in the corresponding reflected channel. Assume that the configuration of IRS can be updated on a per-block basis; let $\theta^t_n\in [0, 2\pi)$ be the phase shift of RE $n$ in block $t$. Assume also that there are a total of $T$ blocks and the channels are invariant throughout. In practice, the value of each phase shift is restricted to a prescribed discrete set
\begin{equation}
\Phi_K = \{0,\omega,\ldots,(K-1)\omega\}\;\;\text{with}\;\;\omega = \frac{2\pi}{K},
\end{equation}
where the set size $K\ge2$.
With the background noise $Z^t\sim\mathcal{CN}(0,\sigma^2)$ and the transmit signal $X^t\sim\mathcal{CN}(0,P)$, the received signal $Y^t\in\mathbb C$ in block $t$ is
\begin{equation}
Y^t = \left(h_0+\sum_{n=1}^{N}h_ne^{j\theta_n^t}\right)X^t+Z^t,
\end{equation}
and the SNR in block $t$ can be computed as a function of the corresponding phase shift array $\bm\theta^{t} = (\theta^{t}_1,\ldots,\theta^{t}_N)$ as
\begin{equation}
\label{SNR}
\mathrm{SNR}(\bm\theta^t) = \frac{P\left|h_0+\sum_{n=1}^{N}h_ne^{j\theta^{t}_{n}}\right|^2}{\sigma^2}.
\end{equation}
Roughly speaking, our goal is to maximize SNR for all the $T$ blocks, which will be mathematically formalized in the context of reinforcement learning in the following section. Again, we emphasize that the channels are unknown \emph{a priori}.
\section{A Reinforcement Learning Perspective of Existing Blind Beamforming Methods}
\label{sec:RL_perspective}
\subsection{Review of Existing Blind Beamforming \cite{Arun2020RFocus, ren2022configuring}}
Should we alter the phase shifts when entering a new block? This question becomes trivial if CSI is already known. Since the channels are constant, we can just optimize the phase shift by solving the SNR maximization problem
\begin{subequations}
\label{eq:SNR_problem}
\begin{align}
 &\underset{\bm\theta}{\text{maximize}} \quad \frac{P\bigg|h_0+\sum_{n=1}^{N}h_ne^{j\theta_n}\bigg|^2}{\sigma^2}
 &\text {subject to} \quad\, \theta_n \in \Phi_K,\quad n=1, \ldots, N,
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
for the first block and fix the solution thereafter. Regarding the SNR maximization with CSI available, a common idea 
\cite{wu2020beamforming,you2020channel,abeywickrama2020intelligent,wang2020intelligent} is to rotate every $h_n$ to the closest position to $h_0$, i.e., 
\begin{equation}
\label{CPP}
\theta^\star_{n} = \min_{\varphi\in\Phi_K}\left|\mathsf{Arg} \left(\frac{h_ne^{j\varphi}}{h_0}\right)\right|,
\end{equation}
where $\mathsf{Arg}$ is the principal argument, namely the closest point projection (CPP). 
\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{CSM blind beamforming method in \cite{Arun2020RFocus, ren2022configuring}}
\label{alg:CSM}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \textbf{Input:} Discrete set $\Phi_K$, number of blocks $T$, number of exploration blocks $T_0$
\For{$t=1,\ldots,T_0$}
 \State Sample $\theta_n^t\sim \mathrm{Uniform}(\Phi_K)$, $\forall n=1,\ldots,N$
 \State Apply $\boldsymbol{\theta}^t=(\theta_1^t,\ldots,\theta_N^t)$ and measure
 $\mathrm{SNR}(\boldsymbol{\theta}^t)$.
\EndFor
\State Compute the CSM $Q_n^{T_0}(\varphi)$ according to \eqref{eq:compute_Q}.
\State Decide each $\theta_n$ according to \eqref{eq:CSM_solution}.
\For{$t=T_0+1,\ldots,T$}
 \State Apply $\boldsymbol{\theta}^t=(\theta_1^{\mathrm{CSM}},\ldots,\theta_N^{\mathrm{CSM}})$.
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}
But the reality is that the CSI is unknown. It turns out that the CPP solution can be recovered implicitly via random sampling. Specifically, as proposed in \cite{Arun2020RFocus, ren2022configuring}, we try out a new sample $\bm\theta^t$ with each $\theta^t_n$ drawn from $\Phi_K$ i.i.d. and then record the corresponding $\mathrm{SNR}(\bm\theta^t)$. After $T_0$ samples, we compute the conditional sample mean (CSM)
\begin{equation}
 \label{eq:compute_Q}
 Q^{T_0}_{n}(\varphi) = \frac{\sum^{T_0}_{i=1}\mathrm{SNR}(\bm\theta^{i})\cdot\mathbbm 1_{\theta^{i}_n=\varphi}}{\sum^{T_0}_{i=1}\mathbbm 1_{\theta^{i}_n=\varphi}}
\end{equation}
for each $\varphi\in\Phi_K$. The blind beamforming method in \cite{Arun2020RFocus, ren2022configuring} is to choose each phase shift $\theta_n$ to maximize the CSM, i.e.,
\begin{equation}
 \label{eq:CSM_solution}
 \theta_n^{\mathrm{CSM}} = \arg\mathop{\max}\limits_{\varphi\in\Phi_K}Q^{T_0}_n(\varphi).
\end{equation}
The main result in \cite{Arun2020RFocus, ren2022configuring} follows:
\begin{proposition}[Theorem 2 in \cite{ren2022configuring}]
 As $T_0\rightarrow\infty$, the solution $\theta_n^{\mathrm{CSM}}$ in \eqref{eq:CSM_solution} tends to the CPP solution $\theta^\star_n$ in \eqref{CPP}.
\end{proposition}
But we cannot let $T_0\rightarrow\infty$ since the total number of blocks $T$ is finite. A practical realization of blind beamforming in our case is to use $T_0<T$ blocks to learn the CSM values $\{Q^{T_0}_n(\varphi)\}$, namely training. For the rest blocks, we decide $\bm\theta^t$ by the blind beamforming method \cite{Arun2020RFocus, ren2022configuring} with the estimated $\{Q^{T_0}_n(\varphi)\}$. Thus, the above blind beamforming method can be recognized as a reinforcement learning approach: \emph{explore} new solutions for the first $T_0$ blocks and \emph{exploit} the CSM so far for the remaining $T-T_0$ blocks.
Furthermore, our problem can be thought of as a multi-armed bandit problem with each possible solution $\bm\theta^t\in\Phi_K^N$ treated as an arm and the received SNR under $\bm\theta^t$ is treated as the reward.
Following this line, we can generalize the CSM blind beamforming method \cite{Arun2020RFocus, ren2022configuring} as an $\epsilon$-greedy algorithm---which is a classical approach to the multi-armed bandit problem. At the beginning of each block $t$, we determine whether the block is used for exploration or exploitation. Specifically, block $t$ is selected as an exploration block with probability $0<\epsilon<1$ and as an exploitation block with probability $1-\epsilon$. Therefore, for each block $t$, the phase shift $\theta_n$ is determined as
\begin{equation}\label{greedy}
\theta_n^{t}=\left\{
\begin{aligned}
&\arg\mathop{\max}\limits_{\varphi\in\Phi_K}Q^t_n(\varphi)\; \text{with probability}\; 1-\epsilon;
&\varphi \sim \mathrm{Uniform} (\Phi_K)\;\text{with probability}\; \epsilon.
\end{aligned}
\right.
\end{equation}
Note that the $Q_n^t(\varphi)$ is now computed as the average SNR over all exploration blocks so far, i.e.,
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:update_Q}
 Q_n^t(\varphi)
=
\frac{
\sum_{\tau=1}^{t-1}
\mathrm{SNR}(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\tau})
\cdot \mathbbm{1}_{\theta_n^\tau=\varphi}
\cdot \mathbbm{1}_{\tau \in \mathcal{T}_t}
}{
\sum_{\tau=1}^{t-1}
\mathbbm{1}_{\theta_n^\tau=\varphi}
\cdot \mathbbm{1}_{\tau \in \mathcal{T}_t}
},
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{T}_t$ is the set of indices of all exploration blocks before block $t$.
The above process is summarized in Algorithm \ref{alg:epsilon_greedy}. In contrast to the CSM blind beamforming method in \cite{Arun2020RFocus, ren2022configuring} that uses the first $T_0$ blocks for exploration, we now decide the use of each block $t$, exploration or exploitation, randomly.
The above interpretation further leads us to the definition of \emph{regret} as considered in the reinforcement learning field. Since 
the CPP solution $\{\theta^\star_n\}$ is the ultimate goal and it can be obtained from $Q^{T_0}_{n}(\varphi)$ with $T_0\rightarrow\infty$, we may consider the following regret for the action $\bm\theta^t$ in block $t$:
\begin{equation}
 G_t = \sum^N_{n=1} \left(Q^\infty_n(\theta_n^\star)-Q^\infty_n(\theta_n^{t})\right).
\end{equation}
It can be shown that $G_t$ must be nonnegative and it equals 0 if $\theta_n^t = \theta^\star_n$ for every $n$. We seek the optimal $\{\bm\theta^{t}\}$ to minimize the expected cumulative regret over the $T$ blocks:
\begin{subequations}
\label{new opt problem}
\begin{align}
 &\underset{\{\bm{\theta}^t\}}{\text{minimize}} \quad \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^{T}G_t\right]
 \label{new opt problem:obj}
 &\text {subject to} \quad \theta_{n}^t \in \Phi_K,\;\text{for each}\;\;(n,t).
 \label{new opt problem:contraint}
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
The $\epsilon$-greedy algorithm in \eqref{greedy} constitutes the standard approach to the above problem in the reinforcement learning field. But we raise two questions: What is the optimal value of $\epsilon$ in \eqref{greedy}? Is there a better way of exploration than the uniform sampling? We will answer the first question in the following subsection and defer the second question to Section \ref{sec:gradient}.
\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{$\epsilon$-Greedy Algorithm}
\label{alg:epsilon_greedy}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \textbf{Input:} Discrete set $\Phi_K$, number of blocks $T$, exploration probability $\epsilon$
\State Initialize $Q_n^1(\varphi)=0$
\For{$t=1,\ldots,T$}
 \State Update $Q_n^t(\varphi)$ according to \eqref{eq:update_Q}.
 \State Decide block $t$ according to the probability $\epsilon$.
 \If{block $t$ is an exploration block}
 \State Sample $\theta_n^t\sim \mathrm{Uniform}(\Phi_K)$, $\forall n=1,\ldots,N$
 \Else
 \State Set $\theta_n^t\in\arg\max_{\varphi\in\Phi_K} Q_n^t(\varphi), \forall n=1,\ldots,N$
 \EndIf
 \State Apply $\boldsymbol{\theta}^t=(\theta_1^t,\ldots,\theta_N^t)$ and measure
 $\mathrm{SNR}(\boldsymbol{\theta}^t)$.
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}
\subsection{Parameter Tuning for $\epsilon$-Greedy Algorithm}
\label{subsec:greedy}
Clearly, there is a trade-off in deciding the value of $0<\epsilon<1$. If $\epsilon\rightarrow0$, then the distortion between the real CSM $Q^\infty_n(\varphi)$ and the estimated CSM  $Q^t_n(\varphi)$ can be large, so the resulting blind beamforming solution is of low quality. Conversely, if $\epsilon\rightarrow1$, then most blocks are used for exploration and consequently SNR should be fairly low for most of the time.
But optimizing $\epsilon$ directly in \eqref{new opt problem} is quite difficult because the objective is difficult to express in a closed form with respect to $\epsilon$. To solve this issue, the following proposition provides an upper approximation of regret that facilitates the optimization significantly.
\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:greedy_bound}
For the $\epsilon$-greedy method, the expected value of the cumulative regret over $T$ blocks is upper bounded as
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:upper_bound}
\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^{T}G_t\right] \leq\rho TN\left(\frac{2K}{T^4}+\epsilon\right)+2N\rho(1-\epsilon )\sqrt{\frac{2KT\log T}{\epsilon}}
\end{equation}
given any parameter $0<\epsilon<1$, where $\rho = \frac{P}{\sigma^2}\left(\sum_{n=0}^{N}|h_n|\right)^2$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{IEEEproof}
We begin with an exploitation block $t$. Defining an event for each $\varphi\in\Phi_K$ as
\begin{equation}
 \label{eq:event}
 \mathcal{E}(\varphi)=\left\{\left|Q_n^t(\varphi)-Q_n^\infty(\varphi)\right|> \rho\sqrt{\frac{2K\log T}{\epsilon\cdot T}}\right\},
\end{equation}
we show that
\begin{align}
\label{eq:pro_event}
&\mathbb{P}\left\{\left|Q_n^t(\varphi)-Q_n^\infty(\varphi)\right|\leq \rho\sqrt{\frac{2K\log T}{\epsilon\cdot T}},\;\forall \varphi\in\Phi_K\right\}\notag 
&= 1-\mathbb{P}\Big\{\bigcup\nolimits_{\varphi\in\Phi_K}\mathcal{E}(\varphi)\Big\}\notag
&\stackrel{(a)}{\geq}1-\sum_{\varphi\in\Phi_K}\mathbb{P}\left\{\mathcal E(\varphi)\right\} \notag 
&\stackrel{(b)}{\geq} 1-\frac{2K}{T^4},
\end{align}
where $(a)$ follows by the union bound while $(b)$ follows by Hoeffding's inequality. Moreover, the exploitation in \eqref{greedy} guarantees that
\begin{equation}
 \label{eq:regret_cond}
Q_n^t(\theta_n^t)\geq Q_n^t(\theta_n^\star).  
\end{equation}
Combining \eqref{eq:pro_event} and \eqref{eq:regret_cond}, we have
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}\left\{Q_n^\infty(\theta^\star_n)-Q_n^\infty(\theta^t_n)\le 2\rho\sqrt{\frac{2K\log T}{\epsilon\cdot T}}\right\}\ge 1-\frac{2K}{T^4}.
\end{equation}
We now consider a general block $t=1,\ldots,T$. Clearly, it always holds that
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:bound_1}
Q^\infty_n(\theta_n^\star)-Q^\infty_n(\theta_n^{t})
\leq \rho.
\end{equation}
Thus, the expected value of $Q^\infty_n(\theta_n^\star)-Q^\infty_n(\theta_n^{t})$ can be bounded from above as
\begin{align}
&\mathbb{E}\left[Q^\infty_n(\theta_n^\star)-Q^\infty_n(\theta_n^{t})\right] \notag 
 &\leq \epsilon\rho+(1-\epsilon)\left(\left(1-\frac{2K}{T^4}\right)2\rho\sqrt{\frac{2K\log T}{\epsilon\cdot T}}+\frac{2K}{T^4}\rho\right) \notag
 &\leq \epsilon\rho+2(1-\epsilon)\rho\sqrt{\frac{2K\log T}{\epsilon\cdot T}} + \frac{2K}{T^4}\rho.
\label{Q:diff}
\end{align}
Summing both sides of \eqref{Q:diff} gives rise to \eqref{eq:upper_bound}.
\end{IEEEproof}
We now use the upper approximation in \eqref{eq:upper_bound} to replace the objective function in \eqref{new opt problem}. The resulting new problem turns out to be convex in $\epsilon$, so it suffices to solve the first-order condition:
\begin{equation}
\label{first order cond}
 \sqrt{\epsilon^3 T^3}-T(1+\epsilon )\sqrt{2K\log T}=0.
\end{equation}
Further, it can be shown that the above first-order equation has only one real root 
\begin{equation}
\label{opt:epsilon} \epsilon^\star=\sqrt{\frac{2K\log T}{9T}}+\sqrt[3]{a+\sqrt{a^2+b^3}} 
 +\sqrt[3]{a-\sqrt{a^2+b^3}},
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
 a&=\left(1+\frac{4K\log T}{27T}\right)\sqrt{\frac{K\log T}{2T}} 
 b&=\frac{2K\log T}{9T}.
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
For the CSM method, if $T_0$ is relaxed to a continuous variable, then we have $T_0^\star=\epsilon^\star T$, since it is a special case of the $\epsilon$-greedy algorithm. However, $T_0$ must be an integer, and thus $T_0^\star$ can be obtained by rounding $\epsilon^\star\cdot T$ to the nearest integer.
\section{Proposed Non-Uniform Sampling}
\label{sec:gradient}
\subsection{Gradient Sampling}
Our discussion thus far is based on uniform sampling---which is questionable since it does not take the past performance into account when choosing a new $\bm\theta^t$ for exploration. For instance, if using a particular phase shift $\varphi\in\Phi_K$ for RE $n$ always results in poor performance, then it is advisable to avoid this setting in future explorations.
As the main contribution of this work, we extend the gradient bandit method in \cite{sutton2018reinforcement} to address the above issue, as stated in what follows. Specifically, we introduce a \emph{numerical preference} variable $H_n^t(\varphi)$ for each $(n,\varphi)$ in block $t$ to characterize the ``goodness'' of setting $\theta^t_n=\varphi$. Roughly speaking, for each RE $n$, if a phase shift option $\varphi \in \Phi_K$ has a larger numerical preference variable $H_n^t(\varphi)$ at block $t$, it will have a higher probability of being sampled in this block. To achieve this, we incorporate the softmax probability
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:prob_phi}
\pi_n^t(\varphi)\triangleq\mathbb{P}\left\{\theta_n^t=\varphi\right\}\triangleq\frac{e^{H_n^t(\varphi)}}{\sum_{\varphi^\prime\in\Phi_K}e^{H_n^t(\varphi^\prime)}},
\end{equation}
and make a soft decision for each $\theta^t_n$ as
\begin{equation}
 \label{eq:distribution}
 \theta_n^t=\varphi\;\text{with probability}\; \pi^t_n(\varphi).
\end{equation}
\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Gradient Sampling Algorithm}
\label{alg:gradient_sampling}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \textbf{Input:} set $\Phi_K$, number of blocks $T$, learning rates $\{\gamma^t\}$
\State Initialize $H_n^1(\varphi)=0,\forall n,\varphi$.
\For{$t=1,\ldots,T$}
 \For{$n=1,\ldots,N$}
 \State Compute
 $\pi_n^t(\varphi),\,\forall \varphi\in\Phi_K$ according to \eqref{eq:prob_phi}.


 \State Decide each $\theta^t_n$ according to \eqref{eq:distribution}.
 \EndFor
 \State Apply $\boldsymbol{\theta}^t=(\theta_1^t,\ldots,\theta_N^t)$ and measure
 $\mathrm{SNR}(\boldsymbol{\theta}^t)$.

 \State Compute $\delta^t$ according to \eqref{eq:delta_t}.

 \State Obtain $H_n^{t+1}(\varphi),\forall n,\varphi$ according to \eqref{eq:update_H}.
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}
It remains to specify how each $H^t_n(\varphi)$ is obtained. By convention, every $H^1_n(\varphi)$ is set to zero. Sequentially, each $H^{t+1}_n(\varphi)$ is computed based on the previous $\{H_n^{t}(\cdot),\pi^{t}_n(\varphi)\}$ and the past rewards as
\begin{equation}
 \label{eq:update_H}
 H_n^{t+1}(\varphi) = H_n^{t}(\varphi) + \gamma^t\cdot g_n^t(\varphi),
\end{equation}
where
\begin{align}
 \label{eq:g_nt}
 g_n^t(\varphi) &= \delta^t\left(\mathbbm{1}\{\varphi = \theta_n^t\}-\pi^t_n(\varphi)\right) \notag 
 &=\begin{cases}
  \delta^t(1-\pi_n^{t}(\theta_n^t)), & \text{if } \varphi=\theta_n^t, 
  -\delta^t\pi_n^{t}(\varphi),  & \forall\varphi\ne\theta_n^t,
\end{cases}
\end{align}
and $\gamma^t>0$ is the learning rate in block $t$. Moreover,
\begin{equation}
 \label{eq:delta_t}
 \delta^t = \mathrm{SNR}(\bm\theta^t) - \overline{r}^{t-1}
\end{equation}
is the difference between the latest reward (which is the SNR value) and the average reward before block $t$:
\begin{equation}
\bar r^{t-1}
\triangleq
\frac{1}{t-1}\sum_{i=1}^{t-1}\mathrm{SNR}(\boldsymbol{\theta}^i).
\end{equation}
By convention, we set $\bar r^{0}=0$. According to \eqref{eq:update_H}, if the SNR under the current sample is higher than the average SNR up to the previous round, we increase the numerical preference of the phase selected for each RE $n$. The process of the proposed gradient sampling algorithm is summarized in Algorithm \ref{alg:gradient_sampling}. Note that we no longer distinguish between exploration and exploitation when applying the gradient sampling algorithm. In the following subsection, we show that the proposed gradient sampling algorithm is an instance of stochastic gradient ascent, thereby guaranteeing its convergence.
\begin{remark}
Although the proposed gradient sampling algorithm is originally designed to maximize the received SNR, it can be readily extended to other objectives. Specifically, let $f(\bm{\theta})$ denote an objective function defined with respect to the IRS configuration $\bm{\theta}$. For a maximization problem, the gradient sampling algorithm can be used by replacing $\mathrm{SNR}(\bm{\theta}^t)$ with $f(\bm{\theta}^t)$ as the reward in block $t$. For a minimization problem, $-f(\bm{\theta}^t)$ can be used as the reward in block $t$. Therefore, the proposed gradient sampling algorithm is applicable to tasks such as SNR reduction and multi-user max--min fairness. We also evaluate the performance of gradient sampling on these tasks in Section \ref{sec:field}.
\end{remark}
\subsection{Convergence Analysis}
This section aims to provide a deeper insight into the gradient sampling algorithm by extending the analysis in \cite{sutton2018reinforcement}. Specifically, we will show that the gradient sampling algorithm is a stochastic approximation of gradient ascent to solve the expected SNR maximization problem, and therefore we can characterize its convergence behavior. 
Let
\begin{equation}
 \mathbf H \triangleq \{H_n(\varphi): n=1,\ldots,N,\ \varphi\in\Phi_K\}
\end{equation}
be the set of all numerical preference variables. We now suppress the block index and write each $H_n(\phi)$ as a generic numerical preference variable; its value at block $t$ generated by Algorithm \ref{alg:gradient_sampling} is denoted by $H_n^t(\phi)$. Given $\mathbf H$, the REs independently sample their phase shifts according to the softmax probabilities in \eqref{eq:prob_phi}. Therefore, the probability of a specific phase-shift vector $\boldsymbol{\theta}=(\theta_1,\theta_2,\ldots,\theta_N)$ is given by
\begin{equation}
\prod_{n=1}^N \pi_n(\theta_n)= \prod_{n=1}^N\frac{e^{H_n(\theta_n)}}{\sum_{\varphi^\prime\in\Phi_K}e^{H_n(\varphi^\prime)}}.
\end{equation}
By treating $\mathbf H$ as the optimization variable, we define the expected SNR as a function of $\mathbf H$:
\begin{align}
R(\mathbf H)
&\triangleq
\mathbb E_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}
\left[\mathrm{SNR}(\boldsymbol{\theta})\right] \notag 
&=\sum_{\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\Phi_K^N}
\left(
\prod_{n=1}^N \pi_n(\theta_n)
\right)
\mathrm{SNR}(\boldsymbol{\theta}).
\label{eq:expected_snr_objective}
\end{align}
Rather than directly solving the SNR maximization problem \eqref{eq:SNR_problem}, the proposed gradient sampling algorithm aims to solve the expected SNR maximization problem:
\begin{equation}
 \underset{\mathbf H}{\text{maximize}}\quad R(\mathbf H).
 \label{eq:actual_problem}
\end{equation}
In other words, the gradient sampling algorithm aims to maximize the expected SNR by optimizing $\bm H$ to adjust the sampling distribution of $\bm{\theta}$, such that phase-shift vectors associated with higher SNR values are sampled more frequently. We remark that the above problem reformulation is reasonable because problem \eqref{eq:actual_problem} is equivalent to the original problem \eqref{eq:SNR_problem} based on the following observation: if $\bm\theta=(\theta_1^\star,\theta_2^\star,\ldots,\theta_N^\star)$ is an optimal phase-shift vector, then we can assign $\mathbb{P}\left\{\theta_n=\theta_n^\star\right\} = 1$ by setting $H_n(\theta_n^\star)=+\infty$. 
We next show that the proposed gradient sampling algorithm is a stochastic approximation of gradient ascent to solve problem \eqref{eq:actual_problem}. It suffices to show that $g_n^t(\varphi)$ in \eqref{eq:g_nt} is an unbiased estimator of the gradient of $R(\bH)$ with respect to the variable $H_n(\varphi)$ in block $t$, as summarized in the following proposition.
\begin{proposition}
For every block $t\geq 1$, RE $n$, and phase shift $\varphi\in\Phi_K$, the gradient sampling update $g_n^t(\varphi)$ in \eqref{eq:g_nt} is an unbiased estimator of the partial derivative of the expected SNR objective \eqref{eq:expected_snr_objective} with respect to the numerical preference variable $H_n(\varphi)$, i.e.,
\begin{equation}
\mathbb E_{\boldsymbol\theta^t}\left[g_n^t(\varphi)\right]
=\left. \frac{\partial R(\mathbf H)}{\partial H_n(\varphi)} \right|_{\mathbf H=\mathbf H^t},
\label{eq:unbiased_coordinate_gradient}
\end{equation}
where $\boldsymbol\theta^t$ is the random phase-shift vector given by the gradient sampling algorithm in block $t$.
\label{prop:unbiased}
\end{proposition}
\begin{IEEEproof}
We first derive the partial derivative of $R(\mathbf H)$ with respect to $H_n(\varphi)$ for an arbitrary RE $n$ and phase shift $\varphi\in\Phi_K$. By the definition of $R(\mathbf H)$ in \eqref{eq:expected_snr_objective}, when the partial derivative is taken with respect to $H_n(\varphi)$, only the $n$th softmax term depends on $H_n(\varphi)$. Therefore,
\begin{align}
\label{eq:partial_H}
\frac{\partial R(\mathbf H)}
{\partial H_n(\varphi)}
&=
\sum_{\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\Phi_K^N}
\mathrm{SNR}(\boldsymbol{\theta})
\left(
\prod_{m\neq n}\pi_m(\theta_m)
\right)
\frac{\partial \pi_n(\theta_n)}
{\partial H_n(\varphi)}.
\end{align}
Based on the softmax probability given in \eqref{eq:prob_phi}, we have
\begin{align}
\label{eq:partial_softmax}
&\frac{\partial \pi_n(\theta_n)}{\partial H_n(\varphi)} \notag
&=
\frac{\partial}{\partial H_n(\varphi)}
\left[
\frac{e^{H_n(\theta_n)}}{\sum\limits_{\theta_n^\prime\in\Phi_K} e^{H_n(\theta_n^\prime)}}
\right] \notag
&=
\frac{
\frac{\partial e^{H_n(\theta_n)}}{\partial H_n(\varphi)}
\sum_{\theta_n^\prime\in\Phi_K} e^{H_n(\theta_n^\prime)}
-
e^{H_n(\theta_n)}
\frac{\partial \sum_{\theta_n^\prime\in\Phi_K} e^{H_n(\theta_n^\prime)}}{\partial H_n(\varphi)}
}{
\left(\sum_{\theta_n^\prime\in\Phi_K} e^{H_n(\theta_n^\prime)}\right)^2
} \notag
&=
\frac{
\mathbbm{1}\{\varphi=\theta_n\} e^{H_n(\theta_n)}
}{
\sum_{\theta_n^\prime\in\Phi_K} e^{H_n(\theta_n^\prime)}
}
-
\frac{
e^{H_n(\theta_n)} e^{H_n(\varphi)}
}{
\left(\sum_{\theta_n^\prime\in\Phi_K} e^{H_n(\theta_n^\prime)}\right)^2
}
\notag
&=
\pi_n(\theta_n)\left(\mathbbm{1}\{\varphi=\theta_n\}-\pi_n(\varphi)\right).
\end{align}
Substituting \eqref{eq:partial_softmax} into \eqref{eq:partial_H} gives
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial R(\mathbf H)}
{\partial H_n(\varphi)}
=
\mathbb E_{\boldsymbol\theta}\left[
\mathrm{SNR}(\boldsymbol{\theta})
\left(
\mathbbm{1}\{\varphi=\theta_n\}
-
\pi_n(\varphi)
\right)
\right],
\label{eq:gradient_coordinate}
\end{equation}
and therefore
\begin{equation}
 \left. \frac{\partial R(\mathbf H)}{\partial H_n(\varphi)} \right|_{\mathbf H=\mathbf H^t}=\mathbb E_{\boldsymbol\theta^t}\left[
\mathrm{SNR}(\boldsymbol{\theta}^t)
\left(
\mathbbm{1}\{\varphi=\theta_n^t\}
-
\pi_n(\varphi)
\right)
\right].
\end{equation}
We then compute the expectation of $g_n^t(\varphi)$:
\begin{subequations}
\label{eq:baseline_decomposition}
\begin{align}
&\mathbb E_{\boldsymbol\theta^t}\left[g_n^t(\varphi)\right] \notag 
&=
\mathbb E_{\boldsymbol\theta^t}\left[
\left(
\mathrm{SNR}(\boldsymbol{\theta}^t)-\bar r^{t-1}
\right)
\left(
\mathbbm{1}\{\varphi=\theta_n^t\}
-
\pi_n^t(\varphi)
\right)
\right]\label{eq:E_gnt_1}
&=
\mathbb E_{\boldsymbol\theta^t}\left[
\mathrm{SNR}(\boldsymbol{\theta}^t)
\left(
\mathbbm{1}\{\varphi=\theta_n^t\}
-
\pi_n^t(\varphi)
\right)
\right] \notag 
&\quad
-
\bar r^{t-1}
\mathbb E_{\boldsymbol\theta^t}\left[
\mathbbm{1}\{\varphi=\theta_n^t\}
-
\pi_n^t(\varphi)
\right].\label{eq:E_gnt_2}
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
Note that we can derive from \eqref{eq:E_gnt_1} to \eqref{eq:E_gnt_2} because $\bar r^{t-1}$ is a constant for the current block $t$.
Furthermore, the probability of $\varphi=\theta_n^t$ is given by $\pi_n^t(\varphi)$. Hence,
\begin{equation}
\mathbb E_{\boldsymbol\theta^t}\left[
\mathbbm{1}\{\varphi=\theta_n^t\}
-
\pi_n^t(\varphi)
\right]
=
\pi_n^t(\varphi)-\pi_n^t(\varphi)
=
0.
\end{equation}
Thus, \eqref{eq:baseline_decomposition} reduces to
\begin{equation}
\mathbb E_{\boldsymbol\theta^t}\left[g_n^t(\varphi)\right]
=
\mathbb E_{\boldsymbol\theta^t}\left[
\mathrm{SNR}(\boldsymbol{\theta}^t)
\left(
\mathbbm{1}\{\varphi=\theta_n^t\}
-
\pi_n^t(\varphi)
\right)
\right].
\label{eq:expect_gnt}
\end{equation}
Comparing \eqref{eq:expect_gnt} with \eqref{eq:gradient_coordinate} proves
\eqref{eq:unbiased_coordinate_gradient}.
\end{IEEEproof}
Proposition \ref{prop:unbiased} reveals that the expected update of the gradient sampling algorithm equals the gradient of the expected SNR with respect to the numerical preference variable. Therefore, the algorithm is an instance of stochastic gradient ascent for solving problem \eqref{eq:actual_problem}. This indicates that the gradient sampling algorithm has good convergence properties, as shown in the following two theorems.
\begin{theorem}[Asymptotic first-order stationarity]
\label{theorem:asymptotic}
Consider the gradient sampling update in \eqref{eq:update_H} where $g_n^t(\varphi)$ is defined in \eqref{eq:g_nt}. If the learning rate $\gamma^t$ satisfies the Robbins–Monro conditions, i.e.,
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:gamma_condition}
 \gamma^t > 0, \quad
\sum_{t=2}^{\infty} \gamma^t = \infty, \quad
\sum_{t=2}^{\infty} (\gamma^t)^2 < \infty,
\end{equation}
then the generated sequence is asymptotically first-order stationary, i.e., for all $n$ and $\varphi$, we have
\begin{equation}
 \lim_{t\to\infty}\mathbb E_{\boldsymbol\theta^t}\left[g_n^t(\varphi)\right] = \lim_{t\to\infty}\left. \frac{\partial R(\mathbf H)}{\partial H_n(\varphi)} \right|_{\mathbf H=\mathbf H^t}=0 \text{ almost surely.}
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}
\begin{IEEEproof}
We prove the theorem by verifying the standard conditions for stochastic gradient convergence in \cite{bertsekas2000gradient}. Specifically, a stochastic gradient ascent recursion is asymptotically first-order stationary if the following four conditions hold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the learning rates satisfy the Robbins--Monro conditions;
\item the stochastic gradient is unbiased;
\item the stochastic gradient has a bounded second moment;
\item the objective function is globally smooth and bounded.
\end{enumerate}
The second condition has been established in Proposition \ref{prop:unbiased}, and the first condition is assumed. Therefore, it remains to prove the last two conditions.
We first verify the third condition. By the definition of $g_n^t(\varphi)$ in \eqref{eq:g_nt}, we have
\begin{equation}
 |\delta^t| \leq \max_{\bm\theta\in\Phi_K^N}\mathrm{SNR}(\bm\theta),
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
 |\mathbbm{1}\{\varphi = \theta_n^t\}-\pi^t_n(\varphi)| \leq 1.
\end{equation}
Therefore,
\begin{equation}
|g_n^t(\varphi)|^2\leq\left(\max_{\bm\theta\in\Phi_K^N}\mathrm{SNR}(\bm\theta)\right)^2<+\infty,
\end{equation}
so the stochastic gradient has a bounded second moment.
It remains to verify the last condition. We first show that $R(\mathbf H)$ is globally smooth. Since the action space $\Phi_K^N$ is finite and each softmax probability $\pi_n(\theta_n)$ is a smooth function of the corresponding preference variables ${H_n}$ with bounded derivatives, $R(\mathbf H)$ is a finite sum of smooth functions with bounded derivatives. Hence, $R(\mathbf H)$ is globally smooth. We then show that $R(\mathbf H)$ is bounded. Clearly, we have
\begin{equation}
0\leq R(\mathbf H)
\leq
\max_{\bm\theta\in\Phi_K^N}\,\mathrm{SNR}(\bm\theta).
\label{eq:R_bounded_above}
\end{equation}
Thus, the proposed gradient sampling algorithm satisfies all four conditions. By Proposition 3 in \cite{bertsekas2000gradient}, the sequence generated by \eqref{eq:update_H} is asymptotically first-order stationary.
\end{IEEEproof}
