\section{Field Tests}
\label{sec:field}
\subsection{Field Test Settings}
We conducted the field tests in an indoor scenario as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:field_test}. Our IRS prototype machine consists of custom-designed printed circuit board (PCB) modules, each comprising a $10\times 10$ array of $100$ meta-atoms. Equipped with dual PIN diodes, each meta-atom is independently programmable to furnish four discrete phase-shift states: $\{0, \pi/2, \pi,3\pi/2\}$. The IRS operates at a center frequency of $3.5$ GHz with a $100$ MHz bandwidth. We employ the NI-USRP X410 SDR to instantiate both the transmitter and the receiver. A directional antenna is deployed at the transmitter, and a single omnidirectional antenna is deployed at the receiver. The system operates using a narrowband transmission scheme, emitting a $3.5$ GHz carrier with a $125$ KHz baseband bandwidth. The default transmit power is set to $P=5$ dBm. Baseband signals are sampled at a rate of $500$ KHz at the receiver. All the computations are conducted on a laptop with a 3.2 GHz CPU and 32 GB RAM.
