Aside from CSM, $\epsilon$-greedy, and the proposed gradient sampling algorithm, we also consider a baseline method called \emph{random max sampling (RMS)}---which replaces the exploitation step in the CSM by using the best phase shift vector in the exploration stage, i.e.,
$\bm\theta^t=\arg\max_{\bm\theta\in\{\bm\theta^1,\ldots,\bm\theta^{T_0}\}} \mathrm{SNR}(\bm\theta).$
We set the learning rate $\gamma^t=\frac{0.005}{\sqrt{T}},\;t=1,2,\ldots,T$ for the proposed gradient sampling method. Regarding the $\epsilon$-greedy algorithm, we obtain $\epsilon^\star$ for the different $T$ values according to \eqref{opt:epsilon} as shown in Table \ref{tab:epsilog_T0_star}; we shall also try out other values of $\epsilon$ for comparison purposes. For the CSM and the RMS methods, we obtain $T_0^\star$ by rounding $\epsilon^\star T$ to the nearest integer; the values of $T_0^\star$ for the different $T$ values are also shown in Table \ref{tab:epsilog_T0_star}.
Moreover, we take $Q_n^{10000}(\varphi)$ as an approximation of $Q_n^\infty(\varphi)$ to compute the cumulative regret.
\subsection{SNR Enhancement Performance}
We start by considering the SNR enhancement performance of different algorithms. Fig. \ref{fig:CRegret} shows the cumulative regrets achieved by the various methods versus the number of blocks $T$. Observe that the RMS method yields the highest cumulative regret among the reinforcement learning methods, and the performance gap gradually increases with $T$. This is because the phase shifts used by RMS in the exploitation blocks are not selected to maximize CSM. In contrast, the CSM algorithm achieves a much lower cumulative regret. In addition, $\epsilon$-greedy slightly outperforms CSM when both methods use the same optimized exploration probability $\epsilon^\star$. It is also worth noting that the gradient sampling method significantly outperforms all other methods, although its objective is not specifically designed to minimize cumulative regret.
