 \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig/SNR_CDF.pdf}
 \caption{CDF of the SNR when $T=3000$.}
 \label{fig:cdf}
\end{figure}
Next, we examine the impact of the exploration probability on the performance of the $\epsilon$-greedy method. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:diff_epsilon}, the cumulative regret is the highest when $\epsilon=0.1$ and $\epsilon=0.9$, which indicates that neither excessive exploration nor excessive exploitation is desirable. The $\epsilon$-greedy method with the optimized $\epsilon^\star$ achieves much better performance than the other choices of $\epsilon$, demonstrating the effectiveness of our strategy for optimizing $\epsilon$ based on the upper-bound approximation of cumulative regret.

Fig.~\ref{fig:avg_SNR} further demonstrates the average SNR across $T$ blocks for the different methods. As $T$ increases from $1000$ to $3000$, all methods show an increasing trend in average SNR, indicating that more blocks provide more observations for identifying effective phase shifts. Among all methods, gradient sampling consistently achieves the best performance. Its average SNR increases from about $19$ dB at $T=1000$ to nearly $23$ dB at $T=3000$. In contrast, RMS achieves the lowest SNR in all cases, suggesting that the phase shifts selected by RMS are less effective for SNR enhancement. CSM and $\epsilon$-greedy achieve comparable performance, but both remain clearly below gradient sampling. For example, when $T=3000$, their average SNR values are about $5$ dB lower than gradient sampling. These results demonstrate the advantage of gradient sampling in identifying high-quality phase shifts.
Fig. \ref{fig:SNR_N} shows the average SNR achieved by different algorithms versus the number of REs $N$. It can be observed that all methods achieve a higher average SNR with more REs. Moreover, gradient sampling consistently achieves the highest average SNR across all values of $N$. Its average SNR increases from about $14$ dB at $N=100$ to about $23$ dB at $N=400$. In comparison, the performance of CSM and $\epsilon$-greedy also improves with $N$, but the improvement is less significant than that of gradient sampling. RMS achieves the lowest performance among the four methods over the entire range of $N$. These results indicate that simply increasing the number of REs is not sufficient; an effective sampling strategy is also needed to fully utilize the enlarged IRS.
Finally, Fig.~\ref{fig:SNR_visual} and Fig.~\ref{fig:cdf} illustrate the block-level SNR performance when $T=3000$. Specifically, Fig.~\ref{fig:SNR_visual} shows how the SNR changes over block index $t$, while Fig.~\ref{fig:cdf} presents the corresponding CDF of the SNR samples. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:SNR_visual}, gradient sampling quickly improves the SNR in the early blocks and then maintains a high SNR of about $25$ dB, indicating that it can rapidly identify high-quality phase shifts. In contrast, CSM and RMS exhibit large fluctuations during the exploration stage and then converge to relatively stable SNR values of about $22$ dB and $19$ dB, respectively. The $\epsilon$-greedy method also improves with $t$, but it suffers from frequent low-SNR blocks due to random exploration. The CDF in Fig.~\ref{fig:cdf} confirms these observations from a distributional perspective. The curve of gradient sampling is shifted farthest to the right, and most of its SNR samples are above $23$ dB, whereas CSM and RMS have a considerable portion of samples below this level. Although $\epsilon$-greedy can reach high SNR values, it has a wider SNR distribution and a longer low-SNR tail.
