\subsection{SNR Reduction and Max-Min Fairness Performance}
We are also interested in the performance of these reinforcement learning methods in other tasks, where SNR is replaced with the corresponding task-specific objectives. We first consider the SNR reduction task in which the reward is now given by $f(\bm\theta^t)=-\mathrm{SNR}(\bm\theta^t)$. Moreover, we increase the transmit power $P$ to $10$ dBm in this task. Unlike the SNR enhancement task, a lower average SNR indicates better performance in this setting. Fig.~\ref{fig:reduction_T} compares the average SNR achieved by different methods under the SNR reduction task. We find that gradient sampling achieves the lowest SNR across all values of $T$, and its average SNR is below $3$ dB when $T=3000$. This result shows that gradient sampling also performs well for SNR reduction. In contrast, CSM and $\epsilon$-greedy maintain much higher average SNR values, mostly around $10$--$12$ dB, and their performance changes only slightly as $T$ increases. RMS still achieves the worst performance. When $T=3000$, the average SNR of RMS is about $8$ dB higher than that of gradient sampling.
Finally, we evaluate the max-min fairness performance of these algorithms in the multi-user broadcast network with $U$ users. In this setting, the worst-case SNR among the users is the reward in each block $t$, i.e., $f(\bm\theta^t)=\min_{u}\mathrm{SNR}_{u}(\bm\theta^t)$. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:SNR_U}, when the number of users increases from $2$ to $4$, the average worst-case SNR of all methods decreases because it becomes more difficult to balance the need for more users simultaneously. However, gradient sampling still maintains a clear advantage over the other methods. For example, when the system serves $4$ users, gradient sampling achieves an average worst-case SNR of about $14.7$ dB, while CSM and $\epsilon$-greedy achieve about $8$ dB, and RMS drops to about $5$ dB. These results show that gradient sampling can also identify high-quality phase shifts for multi-user broadcast transmission.
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
Blind beamforming for IRS without channel estimation is of great practical importance. This work considers blind beamforming from a reinforcement learning perspective, showing that the existing CSM method \cite{Arun2020RFocus, ren2022configuring} can be recognized as a special case of the $\epsilon$-greedy algorithm for the multi-armed bandit problem. We further propose an upper approximation of the cumulative regret and thereby optimize the exploration probability $\epsilon$ in closed form. Moreover, we suggest a gradient sampling scheme that is more efficient than the uniform sampling in the $\epsilon$-greedy algorithm. The convergence of the proposed gradient sampling is also verified by showing that it is a stochastic approximation to gradient ascent. Field tests at the 3.5 GHz real-world testbed demonstrate these reinforcement learning approaches.
