Median imputation was used to provide the remaining missing values, which is suitable due to skewed distributions present in those columns for which missing values occur.
No imputation was performed for one-hot encoded values.
The variables with the most missing values were SpO$_2$ and whether traditional medicine was sought, each missing for only 3.1\
As we would like to determine which of the features listed in Table~\ref{tab:clinical_features} aid a deep-learning based LUS system to triage for TB, we partition them into subsets based on their ease of access.
The first,  class A, includes variables which are easiest to acquire and are self-reported on a questionnaire.
This class therefore is anamnestic in nature and subject to the patient's ability to accurately recall.
The second, class B, includes variables which require some basic measurement, or the intervention of a minimally trained healthcare worker.
Finally, class C includes any value which requires a POC test, and represents the least accessible of the features we consider.
It is important to note that for this study only two POC tests were considered (HIV and malaria) and therefore the conclusions drawn are only with respect to these two tests, and not to POC tests in general.
The division into these classes is relevant because easy-to-acquire variables minimise the barrier to downstream molecular testing and true TB verification.
Therefore, the identification of such features that also improve a deep-learning LUS screening tool reduce the burden on and improve the throughput of the healthcare system.
