\section{Conclusions}
In this work we have considered the effectiveness of incorporating clinical and demographic variables routinely collected during screening into a machine learning-based lung ultrasound (LUS) TB classification system.
Classifiers which leverage only clinical and demographic values which require some basic measurement or are self-reported yield test set AUROCs of 0.88 [0.81,0.96].
No substantial improvement is seen when adding measurements by POC tests (0.89 [0.83,0.96] p=0.61), although this additional set is in our experiments limited to only tests for HIV and malaria.
Feature selection is utilised to identify a subset of the most informative variables, 
thereby reducing the number which must ultimately be captured, and is therefore practically useful.
A simple average-based fusion of the output scores produced by a classifier for the clinical and demographic data and a second classifier for the LUS images is shown to consistently provide improved performance, increasing the test-set AUROC to 0.95 [0.91,0.99].
The average performance of this fused system falls within the WHO minimum screening (0.6 specificity at 0.9 sensitivity) guidelines achieving a specificity of 0.76 [0.63,0.85] and a sensitivity of 0.93 [0.81,0.98].
