If a beamforming mapping is learned from a separate training dataset
$\mathcal D_{\rm train}$, e.g., using a neural network, the learned
mapping is held fixed during calibration and deployment. If the calibration and deployment samples are exchangeable conditional
on $\mathcal D_{\rm train}$, the preceding proof yields
$\mathbb E[\setx S_{N_c+1}(a_{\widehat j})
\mid \mathcal D_{\rm train}]\leq\alpha$
almost surely, where the expectation averages over calibration and
deployment samples. Averaging over $\mathcal D_{\rm train}$ gives \eqref{eq:crc_guarantee_main}.
\begin{proposition}[Least-power CRC-certified margin]
\label{thm:least_power}
For fixed channel estimates $\widehat{\matx H}$, let
$P^\star(\lambda_j;\widehat{\matx H})$ denote the optimal value of
\eqref{eq:margin_bf}, with value $+\infty$ if the problem is
infeasible. This optimal value is non-decreasing in $j$.
Consequently, whenever $\widehat j\leq J$, for every regular margin
$\lambda_j$ satisfying the CRC criterion
\begin{equation}
P^\star(\lambda_{\widehat j};\widehat{\matx H})
\leq P^\star(\lambda_j;\widehat{\matx H}).
\label{eq:least_power_crc}
\end{equation}
Thus, a globally optimal candidate at the selected margin has the
minimum required transmit power within this certified family,
whenever that candidate problem is feasible.
\end{proposition}
\noindent\emph{Proof.}
For $j<m$, $\gamma_k(1+\lambda_j)\leq\gamma_k(1+\lambda_m)$
for every user $k$. Hence, the feasible set at $\lambda_m$ is
contained in that at $\lambda_j$. Minimizing the same power objective
over these nested sets gives
$P^\star(\lambda_j;\widehat{\matx H})
\leq P^\star(\lambda_m;\widehat{\matx H})$,
including infeasible cases under the stated convention.
The selection rule chooses the smallest certified index whenever a
regular margin is certified. Every other certified regular margin
therefore has index at least $\widehat j$, establishing
\eqref{eq:least_power_crc}.
\hfill$\square$
