\section{Conclusion and Future Work}
\label{sec:conclusion}
We proposed CRCBeam, a power-constrained multiuser beamforming
policy that calibrates served-outage risk without constructing channel uncertainty sets. Under exchangeability, it provides finite-sample risk control without a parametric CSI-error model and selects the certified regular margin with the lowest optimal required power on the candidate grid. Numerical experiments on a synthetic channel error model based on intermittent pilot contamination show low served-outage and high availability, supported by increased transmit power. In comparison, Gaussian-ball beamforming exceeds the target risk, while conformal-ball beamforming loses availability at 
stringent targets. These results demonstrate the practical
reliability--power tradeoff enabled by direct risk calibration.
Future work will address non-exchangeable channel evolution and
larger multiuser systems.
