\begin{abstract}
Echocardiography is the most widely used cardiac imaging modality, yet interpretation demands integrating visual evidence across global anatomy, localized structures and dynamic cardiac motion. Machine-learning models have automated individual tasks, but they are typically built for a single purpose and depend on expensively labeled datasets—a barrier particularly acute in pediatric care, where data are scarce and anatomy changes with age. Here we present EchoDino, a self-supervised foundation model for echocardiography, created by adapting the DINOv3 framework to 3.7 million frames from 1.7 million unlabeled pediatric echocardiography videos. With its encoder frozen, EchoDino produces representations that capture global context, local anatomy, and dense spatial detail. We introduce Motion-biased Entropy Maximization Sampling (MEMS) to select the most informative frames for video-level analysis. Across nine pediatric and adult datasets, EchoDino outperformed strong baseline models, raising view-classification accuracy from 0.609 to 0.889 and the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve for structural-heart-disease detection from 0.811 to 0.872, while also cutting age-estimation error from 3.857 to 1.389 years, achieving the best segmentation accuracy and lowering ejection-fraction errors. By generalizing from label-free pediatric data to adult echocardiography, EchoDino offers a versatile foundation for cardiac image analysis across the lifespan. \footnote{Code is available at
\url{https://github.com/Sean-C71/EchoDino_}.}
\end{abstract}
\section{Introduction}
Echocardiography is central to cardiovascular care, offering a portable, real-time window onto cardiac structure and function. That versatility also makes echocardiographic data unusually heterogeneous. A single comprehensive study can combine two-dimensional, multi-cycle cine loops, still frames, color Doppler, M-mode, and sweeps acquired across multiple probe positions and imaging conditions~\cite{EchoBase1,EchoBase2}. The evidence that downstream tasks draw on is just as varied. View classification depends on recognizing the whole field of view; chamber quantification and segmentation demand precise localization of endocardial and myocardial boundaries; and functional or diagnostic assessment often hinges on how those structures move through the cardiac cycle~\citep{EchoDyanmic,EchoPed,CAMUS,echoNetLVH}. Because the heart is a moving, three-dimensional organ imaged across multiple views and modes, a useful representation must capture spatial detail, temporal dynamics, and multimodal context simultaneously. Nowhere is this harder than in pediatric echocardiography, where cardiac structure and function change continuously with growth and the definition of normal shifts across development.
Most current systems manage this complexity by narrowing their scope. Task-specific models are trained for a single view, structure, or clinical endpoint~\citep{EchoDyanmic,EchoPed,CAMUS,echoNetLVH}, while recent generalist models consolidate several predictions through paired reports or predefined supervision~\citep{EchoCLIP,EchoPrime,PanEcho}. These approaches have markedly advanced automated interpretation, but the representations they learn are inherently bounded by the annotations, reporting language, and output scales seen during training. Curating the large labeled datasets they require is prohibitively labor-intensive. Supervised objectives also constrain what a model encodes because they reward features that minimize a fixed clinical loss while discarding subtle anatomical and physiological variation.
Self-supervised learning offers a way past this bottleneck, and early echocardiographic models have exploited it using large collections of individual images or explicit spatiotemporal pretraining~\citep{EchoApex,EchoFM}. It remains unknown, however, whether a single label-free representation pretrained solely on pediatric echocardiograms can simultaneously retain global context, fine-grained anatomy, and temporal information while still transferring to adult examinations. Existing methods often depend on heavy downstream fine-tuning or sample the uneven temporal structure of the cardiac cycle poorly. What is needed instead is a general-purpose foundation model with a frozen encoder whose representations support spatial and temporal tasks across patient ages through lightweight task-specific readouts.
Here we present EchoDino, a self-supervised foundation model for pediatric echocardiography built on the DINOv3 framework~\citep{DinoV3,Gram1,Gram2}. DINOv3 learns global image context and local patch-level features from images alone, without task labels or paired clinical text, which suits echocardiography's multiscale visual structure. Starting from an encoder pretrained on billions of natural images, we continued self-supervised training on 3.7 million unlabeled frames drawn from 1.7 million pediatric echocardiography videos. The encoder remained frozen, while lightweight task-specific readouts were trained for downstream evaluation. The frozen encoder exposes three complementary feature types: a class token that summarizes the whole frame, patch tokens that retain spatially resolved local information, and intermediate features from earlier layers that preserve the boundary detail needed for segmentation (Fig.~\ref{fig:EchoDino_arch}a).
On this shared representation, we build two lightweight extensions that route its spatial and temporal information to the task at hand, rather than acting as separate models. For frame-level prediction, vision transformers conventionally rely on the global class token alone, discarding the spatial geometry required for precise localization and measurement; EchoDino-PATCH recombines the class token with spatially resolved patch tokens to restore that local detail. For video-level prediction, fixed compute budgets are usually met by sampling frames at uniform intervals, a simple but content-agnostic strategy that is ill-suited to the periodic cardiac cycle~\citep{Q-frame}. Because systole and diastole are unequal in length and their balance shifts with heart rate—an effect especially pronounced at the fast, variable rates of children—uniformly spaced frames cover the cycle unevenly and can miss brief but decisive states such as end-systole. Motion-biased Entropy Maximization Sampling (MEMS) instead selects high-motion, feature-diverse frames while using temporal anchors to preserve whole-video coverage. A lightweight transformer then fuses the selected frames while preserving their spatial positions and source-frame timing (Fig.~\ref{fig:EchoDino_arch}b,c).
We evaluate EchoDino across five internal pediatric cohorts, one external pediatric cohort, and three external adult datasets spanning view classification, cardiac measurement, chamber segmentation, age estimation, ejection-fraction (EF) regression, structural heart disease (SHD) detection, and sweep-mode recognition. Although its echocardiography-specific adaptation used pediatric videos alone, EchoDino achieved the best performance among the tested methods on the adult benchmarks for ventricular measurement, chamber segmentation, and ejection-fraction prediction. Under a strictly frozen-encoder protocol, it consistently surpassed the unadapted DINOv3 encoder and generally exceeded the supervised PanEcho and EchoPrime foundation models. EchoDino-PATCH was strongest on localized anatomical targets, while MEMS reduced ejection-fraction error relative to uniform sampling across three independent datasets and retained robust sweep recognition. Together, these results show that a single label-free echocardiography foundation model can support global, localized, dense, and temporally fused predictions across pediatric and adult populations (Fig.~\ref{fig:EchoDino_arch}).
