\subsection{EchoDino aggregates study-level video evidence for robust structural heart disease diagnosis}
\textbf{EchoDino integrates complementary evidence across heterogeneous echocardiographic acquisitions.} Diagnosing SHD requires integrating evidence of pathologic anatomy and physiology across multiple heterogeneous acquisitions and views. We tested whether a lightweight temporal fusion module could aggregate this distributed evidence to distinguish congenital defects from normal anatomy, all while keeping the underlying frame encoder strictly frozen. On the TCH-SHD dataset, we averaged the logits from every video associated with each study. EchoDino achieved an area under the curve (AUC) of 0.872, outperforming PanEcho (0.811) and DINOv3 (0.776) (Table~\ref{tab:tch_chd_disease}). Furthermore, it achieved an F1 score of 0.778 and an accuracy of 0.788, exceeding the strong baselines significantly ($p<0.001$). These robust gains across both ranking and thresholded metrics confirm EchoDino's ability to aggregate complementary spatial evidence for study-level congenital disease detection.
To understand the granularity of this diagnostic accuracy, we performed a sub-analysis of the specific diagnoses within the SHD-positive class. Detailed phenotype-stratified SHD-positive detection rates are reported in Supplementary Note~\ref{si-note:shd-phenotypes} and Supplementary Table~\ref{si-tab:tch_chd_disease_details}. EchoDino achieved the highest or tied rate for all 24 labels, and the uniquely highest rate for 10. Detection reached a perfect 1.000 for 14 critical labels. For the more prevalent atrial septal defect, ventricular septal defect, and patent ductus arteriosus labels, EchoDino achieved robust detection rates of 0.781, 0.856, and 0.790, respectively. By comparison, the strongest baseline model achieved 0.742, 0.733, and 0.730 on these same anomalies. Ultimately, these results demonstrate that EchoDino leverages highly expressive feature representations to effectively aggregate diagnostic evidence across a highly heterogeneous and clinically relevant spectrum of cardiac anatomy.
